WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.490 --> 00:00:08.753
- Welcome everyone to tonight's discussion

00:00:08.753 --> 00:00:12.300
of the current Dangers
to American Democracy,

00:00:12.300 --> 00:00:16.293
sponsored by the Institution
for Social and Policy Studies.

00:00:16.293 --> 00:00:17.910
My name's Alan Gerber.

00:00:17.910 --> 00:00:20.730
I'm the Sterling Professor
of Political Science

00:00:20.730 --> 00:00:23.040
and Director of the Institution

00:00:23.040 --> 00:00:25.050
for Social and Policy Studies.

00:00:25.050 --> 00:00:28.290
And today, I'm honored to share with you

00:00:28.290 --> 00:00:32.939
the insights of our expert
panel on this important topic.

00:00:32.939 --> 00:00:34.255
One thing about ISPS,

00:00:34.255 --> 00:00:36.092
I just wanna remind everyone,

00:00:36.092 --> 00:00:40.080
ISPS is a nonpartisan
research institution.

00:00:40.080 --> 00:00:45.080
And you can go to our website
to find out more about

00:00:45.990 --> 00:00:49.923
our mission, and our
activities, and upcoming events.

00:00:51.630 --> 00:00:55.110
There's gonna be a portion of our events,

00:00:55.110 --> 00:00:57.960
which is question and answer.

00:00:57.960 --> 00:01:01.440
And so, I wanna encourage
you to send questions

00:01:01.440 --> 00:01:03.000
for the panel at any time.

00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:07.410
So, starting now through
the Q&amp;A button at the bottom

00:01:07.410 --> 00:01:09.180
of your Zoom screen.

00:01:09.180 --> 00:01:13.890
And so, we're gonna try and
cover a lot of questions.

00:01:13.890 --> 00:01:16.770
So, keep the questions, if you can,

00:01:16.770 --> 00:01:19.860
brief and to the point.

00:01:19.860 --> 00:01:22.020
And so that'll give us the opportunity

00:01:22.020 --> 00:01:24.780
to answer as many questions as possible.

00:01:24.780 --> 00:01:26.580
And I just also wanna remind everyone

00:01:26.580 --> 00:01:28.950
that today's event is being recorded.

00:01:28.950 --> 00:01:32.400
And we hope to be able to
share a link to this event

00:01:32.400 --> 00:01:34.410
as soon as possible.

00:01:34.410 --> 00:01:37.485
So, now, I wanna introduce
tonight's panelists

00:01:37.485 --> 00:01:40.863
in alphabetical order and briefly.

00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:44.490
Jacob Hacker is the Stanley Resor,

00:01:44.490 --> 00:01:46.950
Professor of Political Science,

00:01:46.950 --> 00:01:49.230
co-director of the Ludwig Program

00:01:49.230 --> 00:01:52.440
in Public Sector leadership
at the Yale Law School,

00:01:52.440 --> 00:01:53.913
and a faculty fellow at ISPS.

00:01:55.032 --> 00:01:59.160
He's an expert on American
politics and policy,

00:01:59.160 --> 00:02:02.280
a member of the American
Academy of Arts and Sciences,

00:02:02.280 --> 00:02:05.730
and the American Academy of
Political and Social Sciences.

00:02:05.730 --> 00:02:09.150
And recently, he created APEX,

00:02:09.150 --> 00:02:11.400
the American Political Economy Exchange,

00:02:11.400 --> 00:02:12.950
which is a new program at ISPS.

00:02:14.006 --> 00:02:18.060
Susan Stokes is the
Tiffany and Margaret Blake,

00:02:18.060 --> 00:02:19.770
Distinguished Service Professor

00:02:19.770 --> 00:02:22.040
at the University of Chicago.

00:02:22.040 --> 00:02:27.040
Sue serves as director of the
Chicago Center on Democracy.

00:02:27.423 --> 00:02:30.390
She's a member of the
National Academy of Sciences

00:02:30.390 --> 00:02:33.390
and the American Academy
of Arts and Sciences.

00:02:33.390 --> 00:02:38.390
And among her just many
important accomplishments,

00:02:38.757 --> 00:02:43.757
Professor Stokes is a
co-founder of Bright Line Watch,

00:02:44.370 --> 00:02:47.178
which is a periodic survey of experts

00:02:47.178 --> 00:02:52.178
and the public on the state
of American democracy.

00:02:52.290 --> 00:02:55.600
And third, we have Milan Svolik.

00:02:55.600 --> 00:02:59.345
Milan is a Professor of
Political Science at Yale,

00:02:59.345 --> 00:03:02.790
and a faculty fellow at ISPS

00:03:02.790 --> 00:03:06.000
who studies the politics
of authoritarian regimes,

00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:09.120
democratization, support for democracy,

00:03:09.120 --> 00:03:11.550
and democratic backsliding.

00:03:11.550 --> 00:03:15.360
His book, the "Politics
of Authoritarian Rule,"

00:03:15.360 --> 00:03:18.450
received the Best Book
Award from the comparative

00:03:18.450 --> 00:03:19.860
democratization section

00:03:19.860 --> 00:03:23.100
of the American Political
Science Association.

00:03:23.100 --> 00:03:26.340
So, with that, we now
turn to our panelists

00:03:26.340 --> 00:03:28.622
who will speak just one
after another without

00:03:28.622 --> 00:03:32.190
additional interruption by me.

00:03:32.190 --> 00:03:36.033
So, with that, Jacob, please take it away.

00:03:36.900 --> 00:03:38.460
- Great, thanks so much.

00:03:38.460 --> 00:03:40.530
So, it's a real pleasure to be here

00:03:40.530 --> 00:03:43.800
and to talk about this
somewhat depressing subject.

00:03:43.800 --> 00:03:48.800
And there is a reason why
I have up this picture

00:03:48.870 --> 00:03:53.870
from a 1980s, don't do drugs commercial.

00:03:56.790 --> 00:04:01.790
That was seared into my
memory as I was a child

00:04:02.820 --> 00:04:04.450
because I used to wake up early

00:04:05.310 --> 00:04:07.140
and watch Saturday morning cartoons.

00:04:07.140 --> 00:04:10.050
And so this announcement,
come on, this is your brain.

00:04:10.050 --> 00:04:11.760
You've probably seen this one before.

00:04:11.760 --> 00:04:12.903
This is your brain.

00:04:15.180 --> 00:04:16.863
This is your brain on drugs.

00:04:17.910 --> 00:04:20.610
You know, I was watching TV you know,

00:04:20.610 --> 00:04:22.680
at 6:00 AM before my parents woke up.

00:04:22.680 --> 00:04:24.960
So, then, I was always
wondering when somebody

00:04:24.960 --> 00:04:26.760
was gonna actually get me breakfast.

00:04:27.600 --> 00:04:28.890
But there's a reason I'm showing this

00:04:28.890 --> 00:04:33.270
because in a lot of ways, I
think that what's happened

00:04:33.270 --> 00:04:37.350
to our democracy can be well
summed up by thinking about

00:04:37.350 --> 00:04:39.872
how it is that a set of polarizing,

00:04:39.872 --> 00:04:44.281
and egalitarian changes in
our political economy have

00:04:44.281 --> 00:04:46.863
changed the way our
political institutions work,

00:04:46.863 --> 00:04:49.500
or as the case may be, don't work.

00:04:49.500 --> 00:04:52.620
So, if you think of the ad
for today, it's, you know,

00:04:52.620 --> 00:04:54.570
here's your political system,

00:04:54.570 --> 00:04:57.750
and this is your political
system on extreme inequality

00:04:57.750 --> 00:04:59.340
and hyperpolarization.

00:04:59.340 --> 00:05:03.369
You take a context, a system,
and a set of institutions

00:05:03.369 --> 00:05:07.770
that was set up in one
era with one set of goals,

00:05:07.770 --> 00:05:10.768
and you transport into it a very different

00:05:10.768 --> 00:05:12.750
set of circumstances,

00:05:12.750 --> 00:05:15.540
and it creates extremely
different outcomes.

00:05:15.540 --> 00:05:19.079
And unfortunately, very,
very unsettling outcomes

00:05:19.079 --> 00:05:20.283
for many of us.

00:05:21.270 --> 00:05:25.230
And so, the way I like to
think about this is that

00:05:25.230 --> 00:05:30.230
our political institutions
we're actually very well suited

00:05:30.480 --> 00:05:32.310
to helping the United States become

00:05:32.310 --> 00:05:36.180
a global economic superpower
in the manufacturing era,

00:05:36.180 --> 00:05:38.272
and actually lead the transition towards

00:05:38.272 --> 00:05:40.730
a modern knowledge economy.

00:05:40.730 --> 00:05:44.400
But those institutions
have proved much less

00:05:44.400 --> 00:05:47.040
well-suited to dealing with the strains

00:05:47.040 --> 00:05:50.343
and dislocations that have
emerged out of that transition.

00:05:51.450 --> 00:05:53.520
And there are two kind
of parts to this story

00:05:53.520 --> 00:05:55.980
that I'm just gonna telescope
here and we can talk about.

00:05:55.980 --> 00:05:59.073
So, the first part is that these strains,

00:05:59.073 --> 00:06:03.780
both the shifts, the social,
and demographic shifts

00:06:03.780 --> 00:06:07.200
and the economic shifts that
have occurred over the last

00:06:07.200 --> 00:06:11.665
30 years or so, have fueled
a great deal of discontent

00:06:11.665 --> 00:06:15.030
and given rise to what Paul Pearson,

00:06:15.030 --> 00:06:18.270
and I have called the Backlash
Coalition in our work.

00:06:18.270 --> 00:06:21.450
And this coalition is not synonymous with,

00:06:21.450 --> 00:06:22.830
but overlaps substantially

00:06:22.830 --> 00:06:25.500
with the contemporary Republican party.

00:06:25.500 --> 00:06:29.350
And what's important is
it includes not just those

00:06:31.170 --> 00:06:32.910
who have been on the kind of front lines

00:06:32.910 --> 00:06:35.280
of the economic changes
that have taken place,

00:06:35.280 --> 00:06:37.209
those who have faced dislocations,

00:06:37.209 --> 00:06:39.330
those who are concerned about

00:06:39.330 --> 00:06:41.100
the changing character of the nation,

00:06:41.100 --> 00:06:43.470
but it also includes a significant portion

00:06:43.470 --> 00:06:46.020
of the American economic elite.

00:06:46.020 --> 00:06:50.730
Paul and I called this
plutocratic populism in our work.

00:06:50.730 --> 00:06:54.360
And the idea is that
although there's a sort

00:06:54.360 --> 00:06:57.030
of common right-wing populous story here,

00:06:57.030 --> 00:06:58.993
there's actually a surprising amount of,

00:06:58.993 --> 00:07:02.940
if not direct support for
this right-wing populism,

00:07:02.940 --> 00:07:07.390
at least and indirect support for it

00:07:07.390 --> 00:07:11.389
from some of the biggest
winners in the economy today.

00:07:11.389 --> 00:07:13.547
Those who benefited the
most from these changes

00:07:13.547 --> 00:07:16.740
that are creating such backlash.

00:07:16.740 --> 00:07:20.684
And the other thing that
I wanna emphasize is that

00:07:20.684 --> 00:07:24.480
our political institutions
have not proven very capable

00:07:24.480 --> 00:07:27.180
of dealing with this
hyperpolarization and inequality.

00:07:27.180 --> 00:07:30.408
And there's kind of two
parts to this story.

00:07:30.408 --> 00:07:34.410
So, first, just on the backlash coalition,

00:07:34.410 --> 00:07:36.979
I think it's really important
to keep in mind that

00:07:36.979 --> 00:07:39.270
we don't have to choose between the kind

00:07:39.270 --> 00:07:43.200
of common economic story
about how the decline

00:07:43.200 --> 00:07:46.151
in manufacturing, the
concentration of prosperity

00:07:46.151 --> 00:07:50.468
in cities has led to discontent.

00:07:50.468 --> 00:07:52.232
And the equally common,

00:07:52.232 --> 00:07:55.198
and I think, also very
persuasive narrative

00:07:55.198 --> 00:07:58.588
that this has a lot to
do with concern about

00:07:58.588 --> 00:08:01.650
the shifts in America's
racial and ethnic makeup,

00:08:01.650 --> 00:08:03.990
and the sense of status
loss that come with it.

00:08:03.990 --> 00:08:05.850
I mean, these are both going on

00:08:05.850 --> 00:08:06.900
and there's a lot of research

00:08:06.900 --> 00:08:08.490
that shows how they come together.

00:08:08.490 --> 00:08:10.830
So, for example, the work of David Otto

00:08:10.830 --> 00:08:12.780
and his colleagues shows that areas hit

00:08:12.780 --> 00:08:16.230
by big manufacturing losses
shift dramatically to the right,

00:08:16.230 --> 00:08:19.567
unless they're relatively diverse,

00:08:19.567 --> 00:08:21.480
in which case they shift to the left.

00:08:21.480 --> 00:08:23.370
But most of the places that have been hit

00:08:23.370 --> 00:08:28.370
have been much less diverse.

00:08:28.500 --> 00:08:30.630
And the other thing I would say is that

00:08:30.630 --> 00:08:32.790
it is pretty important
to keep in mind that

00:08:32.790 --> 00:08:35.400
our media ecosystem, and particularly,

00:08:35.400 --> 00:08:37.230
the prominent role of right-wing media

00:08:37.230 --> 00:08:40.370
that's really important in this
story is pretty distinctive.

00:08:40.370 --> 00:08:44.382
And has really accentuated the nature

00:08:44.382 --> 00:08:46.576
of this backlash and the intensity

00:08:46.576 --> 00:08:49.080
of this backlash coalition.

00:08:49.080 --> 00:08:50.640
The other thing to say
is that this backlash,

00:08:50.640 --> 00:08:51.843
and this will get me to the institutions

00:08:51.843 --> 00:08:53.700
that this backlash coalition

00:08:53.700 --> 00:08:56.250
is concentrated in non-metro areas,

00:08:56.250 --> 00:08:57.529
which are overrepresented,

00:08:57.529 --> 00:08:59.970
as I'll show in our political system.

00:08:59.970 --> 00:09:02.670
Now, I just mentioned that
there's also a big role

00:09:02.670 --> 00:09:04.560
for politically active wealth,

00:09:04.560 --> 00:09:07.400
and I cite a couple studies
here that are very recent

00:09:07.400 --> 00:09:10.740
that show that despite the
shifts that we've seen,

00:09:10.740 --> 00:09:13.740
there still is a large
amount of support among

00:09:13.740 --> 00:09:15.833
the top executive class among corporations

00:09:15.833 --> 00:09:18.450
for the Republican party,

00:09:18.450 --> 00:09:21.090
even as it become more
right-wing populace.

00:09:21.090 --> 00:09:24.330
And so, this poses a
really acute challenge.

00:09:24.330 --> 00:09:28.158
And the first thing that
I just wanna mention

00:09:28.158 --> 00:09:31.306
is that this challenge is reflected in,

00:09:31.306 --> 00:09:34.410
and manifested in the fact that we've seen

00:09:34.410 --> 00:09:36.902
the Republican party move
much more dramatically

00:09:36.902 --> 00:09:39.480
to the right over the last generation

00:09:39.480 --> 00:09:41.499
than we've seen the Democratic
party move to the left.

00:09:41.499 --> 00:09:44.160
And you can see this if you
look across countries that,

00:09:44.160 --> 00:09:45.621
you know, the Democratic party is kind

00:09:45.621 --> 00:09:47.730
of a conventional center left party,

00:09:47.730 --> 00:09:50.520
maybe slightly more center than some.

00:09:50.520 --> 00:09:53.009
The Republican party is
really far to the extremes.

00:09:53.009 --> 00:09:56.223
And I'm sure Sue and Milan
will talk about this,

00:09:56.223 --> 00:09:59.243
but the other thing I
wanna mention is that

00:09:59.243 --> 00:10:01.770
the sort of political institutions

00:10:01.770 --> 00:10:04.407
have accentuated this vulnerability

00:10:04.407 --> 00:10:06.960
or accentuated these trends

00:10:06.960 --> 00:10:09.210
because they have essentially overweighted

00:10:09.210 --> 00:10:11.859
those who are most discontented
with the transformations

00:10:11.859 --> 00:10:15.810
in our society and economy
that are taking place.

00:10:15.810 --> 00:10:17.850
So, there are two pictures here.

00:10:17.850 --> 00:10:21.930
One shows you that you know,
the Senate, as you all know,

00:10:21.930 --> 00:10:25.077
has this bias towards
the Republican party.

00:10:25.077 --> 00:10:27.423
The Republican party
is essentially not one,

00:10:28.260 --> 00:10:31.307
has not represented a majority
of the nation's citizens

00:10:31.307 --> 00:10:36.307
in the Senate since the 1990s.

00:10:36.918 --> 00:10:40.050
But of course, it is held the Senate

00:10:40.050 --> 00:10:41.370
for a lot of this period

00:10:41.370 --> 00:10:45.305
and is very favored to
hold the Senate again soon.

00:10:45.305 --> 00:10:49.854
And it's very difficult to update policies

00:10:49.854 --> 00:10:52.710
to deal with some of the
strains that we're facing

00:10:52.710 --> 00:10:54.275
when there is this like very,

00:10:54.275 --> 00:10:56.344
this system that is very prone to gridlock

00:10:56.344 --> 00:10:58.979
when parties are polarized.

00:10:58.979 --> 00:11:01.814
So, there is this kind
of majoritarian threat

00:11:01.814 --> 00:11:05.782
that's emerging that we see in particular,

00:11:05.782 --> 00:11:08.610
and I think Milan will talk about this

00:11:08.610 --> 00:11:10.880
in the degree to which there's
been very aggressive use

00:11:10.880 --> 00:11:13.110
of state level election administration

00:11:13.110 --> 00:11:16.022
to try to advantage the Republican party.

00:11:16.022 --> 00:11:19.260
So, you can see at the state
level in this picture here,

00:11:19.260 --> 00:11:23.331
that you know, back in
the 1970s, the Democrats,

00:11:23.331 --> 00:11:26.070
if anything, had an edge in gerrymandering

00:11:26.070 --> 00:11:27.630
of state legislative districts.

00:11:27.630 --> 00:11:30.150
Of course, that's shifted
dramatically in their states

00:11:30.150 --> 00:11:31.286
like Wisconsin or Ohio

00:11:31.286 --> 00:11:33.736
that are just very heavily skewed in favor

00:11:33.736 --> 00:11:36.600
of the Republican party despite being

00:11:36.600 --> 00:11:38.206
relatively evenly balanced

00:11:38.206 --> 00:11:40.404
in terms of partisan division overall.

00:11:40.404 --> 00:11:41.426
And of course,

00:11:41.426 --> 00:11:45.341
that feeds into national
political institutions

00:11:45.341 --> 00:11:46.550
because of course,

00:11:46.550 --> 00:11:49.570
this is where districts get
drawn for national politics.

00:11:49.570 --> 00:11:52.440
This is where politicians are drawn from.

00:11:52.440 --> 00:11:54.780
So, I don't feel like I could
do this without at least

00:11:54.780 --> 00:11:59.235
saying a word about it,
whether there's an opportunity

00:11:59.235 --> 00:12:02.940
to escape this vicious cycle.

00:12:02.940 --> 00:12:04.309
And in our prior work,

00:12:04.309 --> 00:12:06.420
Paul and I have written about

00:12:06.420 --> 00:12:07.980
the role of business quite a bit

00:12:07.980 --> 00:12:10.890
and argued that if business
which once was a much more

00:12:10.890 --> 00:12:13.770
moderate force in our politics
were to do that again,

00:12:13.770 --> 00:12:15.690
that that would be really beneficial.

00:12:15.690 --> 00:12:17.970
I have to say I don't see that happening

00:12:17.970 --> 00:12:19.830
and I'm not sure it
will have as much effect

00:12:19.830 --> 00:12:21.090
as we once thought it would,

00:12:21.090 --> 00:12:24.690
given how far the Republican
party has moved to the right,

00:12:24.690 --> 00:12:27.423
and toward a kind of populist stance.

00:12:28.320 --> 00:12:30.436
Ultimately, right, I think,

00:12:30.436 --> 00:12:34.634
the right corrective in our
system would be electoral.

00:12:34.634 --> 00:12:37.740
And there's a lot of talk
about the fact that Republican

00:12:37.740 --> 00:12:40.050
party is kind of exhausting its capacity

00:12:40.050 --> 00:12:41.700
to mobilize its voters.

00:12:41.700 --> 00:12:43.350
But of course, there's this real challenge

00:12:43.350 --> 00:12:48.350
of whether or not the,
there'll be able to be enough

00:12:50.573 --> 00:12:53.304
of a response given the
bias of the institutions

00:12:53.304 --> 00:12:55.487
to see really corrective efforts

00:12:55.487 --> 00:12:59.670
as opposed to further
erosion of democracy.

00:12:59.670 --> 00:13:03.270
And so, as I say at the end,
I think that this is an area

00:13:03.270 --> 00:13:06.173
where you really need to
see broader mobilization

00:13:06.173 --> 00:13:09.960
around electoral and economic reform,

00:13:09.960 --> 00:13:11.434
and political reform.

00:13:11.434 --> 00:13:15.296
And in that, as, A. Philip
Randolph famously said,

00:13:15.296 --> 00:13:17.730
"Organization would be critical."

00:13:17.730 --> 00:13:18.563
Thank you.

00:13:19.740 --> 00:13:21.960
- Hi, everybody, thank you for coming.

00:13:21.960 --> 00:13:23.010
It's great to see you.

00:13:23.010 --> 00:13:26.008
And I'm delighted to follow

00:13:26.008 --> 00:13:29.250
on Jacob's really
interesting presentation.

00:13:29.250 --> 00:13:32.260
I think that there's quite
a bit of overlap between

00:13:33.411 --> 00:13:34.740
his insights and mine,

00:13:34.740 --> 00:13:36.810
which I'm gratified because
he knows a hell of a lot more

00:13:36.810 --> 00:13:38.643
about American politics than I do.

00:13:39.510 --> 00:13:42.270
I'm gonna put some of this in a slightly

00:13:42.270 --> 00:13:43.975
more comparative framework.

00:13:43.975 --> 00:13:46.260
I'm a comparative politics person.

00:13:46.260 --> 00:13:47.093
So, basically, you know,

00:13:47.093 --> 00:13:50.143
what we've seen in the last
eight years or so in the US

00:13:50.143 --> 00:13:52.710
is what we call democratic erosion

00:13:52.710 --> 00:13:54.150
or democratic backsliding.

00:13:54.150 --> 00:13:58.796
And that, basically,
means that the US like,

00:13:58.796 --> 00:14:01.260
about two dozen other countries,

00:14:01.260 --> 00:14:04.320
democracies around the world
has experienced a decline

00:14:04.320 --> 00:14:06.329
in both horizontal accountability,

00:14:06.329 --> 00:14:11.329
which means the ability of
coequal branches of government

00:14:12.000 --> 00:14:15.731
to monitor and influence the actions

00:14:15.731 --> 00:14:17.860
of the executive branch.

00:14:17.860 --> 00:14:19.546
And vertical accountability,

00:14:19.546 --> 00:14:23.727
by which we mean the ability
of voters to be well-informed,

00:14:23.727 --> 00:14:27.115
and to make use elections
to make decisions

00:14:27.115 --> 00:14:29.849
about who their leaders are going to be.

00:14:29.849 --> 00:14:34.284
So, despite the fact that in 2020,

00:14:34.284 --> 00:14:37.230
Donald Trump was defeated,

00:14:37.230 --> 00:14:40.650
American democracy remains fragile.

00:14:40.650 --> 00:14:41.910
And that isn't just

00:14:41.910 --> 00:14:46.170
because a seemingly more
anti-democratic version

00:14:46.170 --> 00:14:48.310
of the former president is poised

00:14:48.310 --> 00:14:51.930
to become the GOP's
presidential candidate.

00:14:51.930 --> 00:14:56.883
It's also because the
structural causes of erosion

00:14:56.883 --> 00:14:58.132
have not disappeared.

00:14:58.132 --> 00:15:03.132
And it's had enduring effects
that also continue to,

00:15:03.601 --> 00:15:04.591
you know, persist,

00:15:04.591 --> 00:15:09.591
and probably make further
erosion that much more likely.

00:15:09.840 --> 00:15:10.673
So,

00:15:22.933 --> 00:15:25.727
(clears throat) just to put
what's happened in the US

00:15:25.727 --> 00:15:28.500
in a somewhat more international context,

00:15:28.500 --> 00:15:31.814
the world has experienced
a wave of backsliding

00:15:31.814 --> 00:15:33.251
since the late 1990s.

00:15:33.251 --> 00:15:35.974
And this figure is a sort
of a illustration of that.

00:15:35.974 --> 00:15:37.564
So, the light blue line

00:15:37.564 --> 00:15:42.564
is the numbers of coups
happening per year,

00:15:43.080 --> 00:15:45.324
and the white line tracks the number

00:15:45.324 --> 00:15:50.324
of eroding events each year.

00:15:50.460 --> 00:15:53.601
So, and the instigators of the backsliding

00:15:53.601 --> 00:15:56.190
come in two basic flavors.

00:15:56.190 --> 00:16:00.330
One is right-wing ethno-nationalists,

00:16:00.330 --> 00:16:05.100
people like Trump and Victor
Orban of Hungary and elsewhere.

00:16:05.100 --> 00:16:07.260
And the other are leftwing populists,

00:16:07.260 --> 00:16:10.735
people like Andres Manuel
Lopez Obrador in Mexico

00:16:10.735 --> 00:16:13.383
or Jacob Zuma in South Africa.

00:16:14.460 --> 00:16:16.980
So, focusing now on
the wealthy democracies

00:16:16.980 --> 00:16:18.990
as is the United States, obviously,

00:16:18.990 --> 00:16:22.890
why have they experienced
an upsurge in right-wing

00:16:22.890 --> 00:16:25.459
ethno-nationalist parties?

00:16:25.459 --> 00:16:28.803
Well, you know, with a great
deal of simplification,

00:16:30.210 --> 00:16:33.289
I'm depicting the stances
of major political parties

00:16:33.289 --> 00:16:36.031
in a lot of, you know,

00:16:36.031 --> 00:16:38.700
this sort of describes
I think in general terms

00:16:38.700 --> 00:16:41.460
a lot of wealthy democracies,

00:16:41.460 --> 00:16:43.860
and how they've kind of array themselves

00:16:43.860 --> 00:16:46.162
in the past couple decades.

00:16:46.162 --> 00:16:50.400
I'm depicting their stances
on three dimensions.

00:16:50.400 --> 00:16:54.496
The vertical dimension is sort
of a traditional large state,

00:16:54.496 --> 00:16:57.710
small state, you know,
heavy taxes, light taxes,

00:16:57.710 --> 00:17:00.367
one the horizontal dimension,

00:17:00.367 --> 00:17:04.590
it has to do with economic
nationalism or globalism.

00:17:04.590 --> 00:17:08.220
And then, the sort of the front
to back dimension is about

00:17:08.220 --> 00:17:09.960
national identities and the extent

00:17:09.960 --> 00:17:12.390
to which those are salient

00:17:12.390 --> 00:17:13.710
in the position of political party

00:17:13.710 --> 00:17:17.770
and defined in kind of
exclusive or inclusive terms.

00:17:17.770 --> 00:17:18.671
In the context

00:17:18.671 --> 00:17:22.200
of the later 20th century
de-industrialization,

00:17:22.200 --> 00:17:25.559
left parties have shifted
toward more globalist

00:17:25.559 --> 00:17:28.759
and somewhat less redistributive stances.

00:17:28.759 --> 00:17:31.530
So, that's depicted here as
the sort of the difference

00:17:31.530 --> 00:17:34.788
between what I'm calling the
legacy left and the new left.

00:17:34.788 --> 00:17:39.608
And in so doing, they
had incentives to do so.

00:17:39.608 --> 00:17:41.248
I won't go into why,

00:17:41.248 --> 00:17:44.251
but a result of this shift has been

00:17:44.251 --> 00:17:47.430
that their brand as parties
of the working class

00:17:47.430 --> 00:17:52.133
became somewhat diluted. (clears throat)

00:17:56.092 --> 00:17:58.976
The shift in a sense opened up a space

00:17:58.976 --> 00:18:02.340
in the southwest and
front corner of this cube

00:18:02.340 --> 00:18:04.770
occupied by the ethno-nationalist, right?

00:18:04.770 --> 00:18:07.560
So, think of the mega faction
of the Republican party

00:18:07.560 --> 00:18:09.557
in the US, Brexiters in the UK,

00:18:09.557 --> 00:18:14.343
Sweden Democrats, and many
other parties of this ilk.

00:18:15.360 --> 00:18:18.259
Not all of them have gained power

00:18:18.259 --> 00:18:21.606
and some of them have gained power,

00:18:21.606 --> 00:18:24.912
that's not led to an erosion
of their democracies.

00:18:24.912 --> 00:18:26.974
When they get access to power,

00:18:26.974 --> 00:18:30.018
why do some of them erode
democracies and not others?

00:18:30.018 --> 00:18:31.276
So, a key risk factor,

00:18:31.276 --> 00:18:34.638
and this is very much in line
with what Jacob was saying,

00:18:34.638 --> 00:18:36.919
a key risk factor that we're finding

00:18:36.919 --> 00:18:39.602
in sort of cross national
statistical research

00:18:39.602 --> 00:18:41.493
is income inequality.

00:18:42.734 --> 00:18:47.734
So, this figure draws
from a cross national

00:18:49.209 --> 00:18:51.493
regression analysis

00:18:51.493 --> 00:18:55.740
where the dependent variable
is the probability of erosion

00:18:55.740 --> 00:18:58.530
and happening beginning in any given year.

00:18:58.530 --> 00:19:01.590
And the horizontal axis is genie,

00:19:01.590 --> 00:19:02.844
the genie coefficient,

00:19:02.844 --> 00:19:05.280
which is a measure of income inequality.

00:19:05.280 --> 00:19:08.849
In this case, it's
post-tax, post transfer.

00:19:08.849 --> 00:19:12.650
And you know what you're
basically seeing here

00:19:12.650 --> 00:19:16.350
is that there is a, you
know, a strong upward trend

00:19:16.350 --> 00:19:18.360
as countries become more unequal.

00:19:18.360 --> 00:19:20.892
The possibilities of
probabilities of erosion

00:19:20.892 --> 00:19:21.916
become stronger.

00:19:21.916 --> 00:19:24.460
Obviously, there are a lot
of other factors going on,

00:19:24.460 --> 00:19:27.030
and there are other things
that I would mention.

00:19:27.030 --> 00:19:29.100
One of them being a kind of contagion

00:19:29.100 --> 00:19:31.257
or demonstration effect that we also see.

00:19:31.257 --> 00:19:32.940
And I think it helps to account

00:19:32.940 --> 00:19:36.179
for the kind of the wave
function that we saw earlier.

00:19:36.179 --> 00:19:38.850
But what you see here, you
know, at the highest level

00:19:38.850 --> 00:19:40.920
of income inequality countries

00:19:40.920 --> 00:19:43.100
like South Africa and Botswana,

00:19:43.100 --> 00:19:47.574
the probability of erosion
rises to around 30%.

00:19:47.574 --> 00:19:49.710
In countries that are very equal,

00:19:49.710 --> 00:19:52.650
so, you know, think the
Scandinavian country, Sweden,

00:19:52.650 --> 00:19:55.165
other Northern European countries,

00:19:55.165 --> 00:20:00.165
the probability drops to
the low single digits.

00:20:00.814 --> 00:20:03.960
Our calculation for the US was about,

00:20:03.960 --> 00:20:08.202
in 2017, was about 9%, which
isn't a very big number,

00:20:08.202 --> 00:20:12.068
but it's a terrifyingly big
number as an annual probability

00:20:12.068 --> 00:20:14.703
of the beginning of an erosion event.

00:20:15.600 --> 00:20:18.718
And it's also kind of an
order of magnitude larger

00:20:18.718 --> 00:20:20.856
than the calculations of the probability

00:20:20.856 --> 00:20:22.760
of democratic instability

00:20:22.760 --> 00:20:27.760
when scholars kind of plugged
the US characteristics in that

00:20:28.590 --> 00:20:33.590
period into old models that were designed

00:20:33.613 --> 00:20:38.130
to explain military coups.

00:20:38.130 --> 00:20:38.963
Okay.
- So, I'm sorry.

00:20:38.963 --> 00:20:43.709
could you define a
democratic erosion event

00:20:43.709 --> 00:20:45.900
just to kind of give folks a sense

00:20:45.900 --> 00:20:47.730
of what you're talking about,

00:20:47.730 --> 00:20:50.490
some concrete examples of what you mean?

00:20:50.490 --> 00:20:55.370
- Yeah, so, actually our measure of this

00:20:55.370 --> 00:20:58.246
heavily depends on work by Melis Laebens

00:20:58.246 --> 00:21:00.960
who was a graduate student
in her dissertation

00:21:00.960 --> 00:21:02.946
that she wrote at Yale a few years ago.

00:21:02.946 --> 00:21:05.793
And so, what she stresses is this,

00:21:05.793 --> 00:21:09.362
I mentioned at the
outset a kind of decline

00:21:09.362 --> 00:21:13.045
in both horizontal and
vertical accountability.

00:21:13.045 --> 00:21:14.839
And concretely,

00:21:14.839 --> 00:21:18.645
we measured this with data
from varieties of democracy,

00:21:18.645 --> 00:21:22.186
which is a Swedish
organization that collects data

00:21:22.186 --> 00:21:24.240
on all kinds of things
related to democracy

00:21:24.240 --> 00:21:25.500
and other kinds of governments.

00:21:25.500 --> 00:21:27.900
So, basically, if you
already are a democracy,

00:21:27.900 --> 00:21:30.300
meaning that you've had
a peaceful transition

00:21:30.300 --> 00:21:32.151
of power sometime in the last five years,

00:21:32.151 --> 00:21:36.229
and you experience a certain
a large enough decline

00:21:36.229 --> 00:21:39.402
in measures of horizontal
and vertical accountability,

00:21:39.402 --> 00:21:44.343
you get counted as that
would be the beginning

00:21:46.110 --> 00:21:48.123
of a kind of a period of erosion.

00:21:49.137 --> 00:21:51.842
So, this includes countries,
you know, kind of familiar set

00:21:51.842 --> 00:21:54.943
of countries like Hungary under Orban,

00:21:54.943 --> 00:21:58.230
as I mentioned, Poland until recent,

00:21:58.230 --> 00:22:01.443
then, the polish under
the peace governments,

00:22:02.460 --> 00:22:03.730
the US under Trump,

00:22:03.730 --> 00:22:08.730
the Mexico under the
current government, Bolivia.

00:22:08.909 --> 00:22:11.956
These are now talking more about cases of,

00:22:11.956 --> 00:22:14.748
as I mentioned before, sort
of left wing populist erosion.

00:22:14.748 --> 00:22:16.800
I'm happy to answer more
questions about that

00:22:16.800 --> 00:22:17.910
if that would be helpful.

00:22:17.910 --> 00:22:22.910
Okay, so, I alluded earlier
to three wealthy democracies,

00:22:24.900 --> 00:22:29.900
all have nurtured right-wing
ethno-nationalist parties.

00:22:31.050 --> 00:22:32.880
Only the United States,

00:22:32.880 --> 00:22:34.932
by the method that I just described counts

00:22:34.932 --> 00:22:38.619
in our calculations and
in most people's books

00:22:38.619 --> 00:22:41.779
is a case among these
three of democratic erosion

00:22:41.779 --> 00:22:44.253
beginning in 2017.

00:22:45.270 --> 00:22:47.520
The UK was kind of a close call.

00:22:47.520 --> 00:22:51.100
And it is a country where income
inequality has risen a lot

00:22:52.807 --> 00:22:54.652
since the last decades of the 20th century

00:22:54.652 --> 00:22:56.967
and approaches the level of the US,

00:22:56.967 --> 00:22:58.980
and it was a kind of a close call.

00:22:58.980 --> 00:23:00.510
And I can talk more about why.

00:23:00.510 --> 00:23:02.700
So, there were elements
of democratic erosion,

00:23:02.700 --> 00:23:05.528
but not to the degree that
we saw in the United States.

00:23:05.528 --> 00:23:07.320
And then, Sweden is another country

00:23:07.320 --> 00:23:09.180
where there's a very powerful,

00:23:09.180 --> 00:23:10.980
and very electorally successful right-wing

00:23:10.980 --> 00:23:11.970
ethno-nationalist party,

00:23:11.970 --> 00:23:13.950
which is now sort of implicitly part

00:23:13.950 --> 00:23:15.424
of a governing coalition.

00:23:15.424 --> 00:23:18.674
But there's no democratic
erosion in Sweden,

00:23:18.674 --> 00:23:21.858
there's no challenge to the
integrity of the judiciary;

00:23:21.858 --> 00:23:23.610
there's no questioning of elections

00:23:23.610 --> 00:23:28.610
as mechanisms of vertical accountability.

00:23:28.937 --> 00:23:32.218
The press doesn't get beaten
up and so on, and so forth.

00:23:32.218 --> 00:23:35.319
Okay, so, why does
inequality encourage erosion?

00:23:35.319 --> 00:23:37.474
I'm probably running low on time.

00:23:37.474 --> 00:23:40.257
Let me just say very
briefly that one reason,

00:23:40.257 --> 00:23:42.037
and this is something that Milan

00:23:42.037 --> 00:23:46.116
can talk a lot more
about is that countries

00:23:46.116 --> 00:23:47.857
that are polarized by income

00:23:47.857 --> 00:23:51.180
also tend to have more polarized politics.

00:23:51.180 --> 00:23:53.970
And for reasons that
Milan and his colleagues

00:23:53.970 --> 00:23:55.259
have explained very well,

00:23:55.259 --> 00:23:58.950
polarization tends to
encourage democratic erosion

00:23:58.950 --> 00:24:01.903
because voters are more
willing to sort of say,

00:24:01.903 --> 00:24:04.590
"Yeah, I don't really like
the fact that this guy

00:24:04.590 --> 00:24:05.815
is beating up on the press

00:24:05.815 --> 00:24:08.237
or undermining judicial independence,

00:24:08.237 --> 00:24:11.565
but God forbid that the
opposition come to power."

00:24:11.565 --> 00:24:12.859
So, that's one reason.

00:24:12.859 --> 00:24:17.044
And you can see here that
these countries sort of line up

00:24:17.044 --> 00:24:19.290
in terms of the level of polarization.

00:24:19.290 --> 00:24:21.181
Again, this is coming from VDM data.

00:24:21.181 --> 00:24:24.963
A second reason has to do
with what we're calling,

00:24:26.248 --> 00:24:31.248
so, the effect of income
inequality on a kind of cynicism

00:24:31.472 --> 00:24:35.922
or skepticism about the ability
of political institutions

00:24:35.922 --> 00:24:38.662
to make any real difference
in people's lives.

00:24:38.662 --> 00:24:40.830
And this is kind of a mood that again,

00:24:40.830 --> 00:24:44.702
is kind of encouraged
by backsliding leaders,

00:24:44.702 --> 00:24:49.420
and leads to what we're
calling institutional nihilism.

00:24:49.420 --> 00:24:53.070
And you can see that we're
finding that in, you know,

00:24:53.070 --> 00:24:55.530
sort of measures of
institutional nihilism agreement

00:24:55.530 --> 00:24:56.530
to survey questions like,

00:24:56.530 --> 00:24:59.040
do you agree that we
cannot fix the problems

00:24:59.040 --> 00:25:00.720
in our political institutions?

00:25:00.720 --> 00:25:02.670
We need to tear them down and start over.

00:25:02.670 --> 00:25:04.050
And when you expose people to,

00:25:04.050 --> 00:25:07.260
you remind people about how
unequal their country is,

00:25:07.260 --> 00:25:09.270
the US, and we did this in Mexico as well,

00:25:09.270 --> 00:25:10.920
you tend to get higher levels of agreement

00:25:10.920 --> 00:25:13.301
with those kinds of responses.

00:25:13.301 --> 00:25:17.433
So, just to finish up, if you know,

00:25:19.650 --> 00:25:21.180
if we wanna have heroes
who are gonna go out

00:25:21.180 --> 00:25:23.452
and fight against democratic erosion,

00:25:23.452 --> 00:25:25.829
we've got our current
president who's made a priority

00:25:25.829 --> 00:25:28.058
of trying to convince people

00:25:28.058 --> 00:25:30.540
that democracy is really worth it.

00:25:30.540 --> 00:25:32.492
But the other guy here is Sean Fain,

00:25:32.492 --> 00:25:36.027
who is the leader of the UAW.

00:25:36.027 --> 00:25:39.569
And he and other labor
leaders have have, you know,

00:25:39.569 --> 00:25:43.804
led us into a period of some
relative successes in strikes,

00:25:43.804 --> 00:25:48.577
and in the ability of unions
to begin to chip away at this,

00:25:48.577 --> 00:25:50.905
you know, kind of
extreme income inequality

00:25:50.905 --> 00:25:52.920
that our country has experienced,

00:25:52.920 --> 00:25:55.206
and that contributes to the erosion

00:25:55.206 --> 00:25:57.600
of democratic institutions here.

00:25:57.600 --> 00:25:59.553
So, with that, I will close.

00:26:01.757 --> 00:26:06.757
- It's an honor to continue
here, and join Sue, and Jacob

00:26:07.355 --> 00:26:12.030
in offering my perspective
on how to diagnose

00:26:12.030 --> 00:26:15.900
what might be the dangers
to the US democracy.

00:26:15.900 --> 00:26:19.283
I would like to do so by
introducing a few elements

00:26:19.283 --> 00:26:22.626
of a study that I did
jointly with Matt Graham,

00:26:22.626 --> 00:26:24.930
who is our former graduate,

00:26:24.930 --> 00:26:28.608
and currently, an assistant
professor at Temple University.

00:26:28.608 --> 00:26:33.180
We were trying to figure
out to what extent Americans

00:26:33.180 --> 00:26:36.991
are willing to prioritize
democratic principles

00:26:36.991 --> 00:26:39.210
over partisan ends.

00:26:39.210 --> 00:26:43.170
And crucially, also, to figure
out what do they believe

00:26:43.170 --> 00:26:46.200
about others' commitment
to democratic principles,

00:26:46.200 --> 00:26:49.290
and whether those beliefs
are warranted in facts.

00:26:49.290 --> 00:26:51.606
And here, I'm showing you a slide

00:26:51.606 --> 00:26:55.230
that summarizes one
scenario that represented

00:26:55.230 --> 00:26:57.822
a representative sample of Americans with,

00:26:57.822 --> 00:26:59.670
and we were trying to figure out,

00:26:59.670 --> 00:27:01.680
we're trying to find a way to gauge

00:27:01.680 --> 00:27:04.020
their commitment to democracy
based on this scenario.

00:27:04.020 --> 00:27:08.002
So, the scenario for instance,
summarizes a situation,

00:27:08.002 --> 00:27:11.730
a real-world situation
that happened in Missouri.

00:27:11.730 --> 00:27:12.563
So, this was a study

00:27:12.563 --> 00:27:16.350
that we conducted both in 2020 and 2022.

00:27:16.350 --> 00:27:18.870
And this is a scenario from 2020

00:27:18.870 --> 00:27:22.009
where the Missouri legislature
was considering changing

00:27:22.009 --> 00:27:24.420
how it does redistricting.

00:27:24.420 --> 00:27:26.430
And there were two groups advocating

00:27:26.430 --> 00:27:27.750
for different ways of doing it.

00:27:27.750 --> 00:27:32.340
The status quo was that there's
a nonpartisan demographer,

00:27:32.340 --> 00:27:35.130
but there was also another
group called Fair Missouri

00:27:35.130 --> 00:27:38.940
that was advocating for
the return of the ability

00:27:38.940 --> 00:27:41.553
to direct redistricting
to the state legislature,

00:27:41.553 --> 00:27:43.530
which was controlled by Republicans.

00:27:43.530 --> 00:27:46.983
So, in effect, partisan
redistricting by Republicans.

00:27:47.850 --> 00:27:49.620
So, this is a real-world scenario

00:27:49.620 --> 00:27:52.104
and we asked respondents to decide

00:27:52.104 --> 00:27:54.514
how they would allocate 10 cents

00:27:54.514 --> 00:27:56.880
between these two groups.

00:27:56.880 --> 00:27:59.670
What we're interested in
is to what extent people

00:27:59.670 --> 00:28:01.901
are willing to allocate
the money to the group

00:28:01.901 --> 00:28:04.110
that advocates to more democratic,

00:28:04.110 --> 00:28:07.480
the less partisan, what we
call the principled option,

00:28:07.480 --> 00:28:10.260
in this scenario that's Clean Missouri,

00:28:10.260 --> 00:28:12.750
that advocated for
nonpartisan redistricting.

00:28:12.750 --> 00:28:15.960
As opposed to one that may
advance their partisan interests.

00:28:15.960 --> 00:28:19.260
In this case, if you're a
Republican, you face a trade-off

00:28:19.260 --> 00:28:22.170
between an option that would allow you

00:28:22.170 --> 00:28:23.850
to further your partisan interests,

00:28:23.850 --> 00:28:26.733
but crucially at the expense of democracy.

00:28:27.930 --> 00:28:32.610
And so, let me show you
what happened depending

00:28:32.610 --> 00:28:36.450
on whether you were faced with
such a partisan trade-off,

00:28:36.450 --> 00:28:38.671
or such a trade-off between partisanship

00:28:38.671 --> 00:28:40.710
and democratic principles.

00:28:40.710 --> 00:28:43.154
And the first, maybe
illustrate what's going on

00:28:43.154 --> 00:28:47.060
is I'll show you what the
Democrats did in this scenario.

00:28:47.060 --> 00:28:48.120
In this scenario,

00:28:48.120 --> 00:28:50.081
the Democrats, both partisan,

00:28:50.081 --> 00:28:53.400
and democratic interests are clear here.

00:28:53.400 --> 00:28:55.890
That is they don't want
Republicans to be able

00:28:55.890 --> 00:28:56.723
to gerrymander,

00:28:56.723 --> 00:28:59.742
and so they should donate
most of their money

00:28:59.742 --> 00:29:00.575
to Clean Missouri,

00:29:00.575 --> 00:29:03.891
which is advocating for
nonpartisan redistricting.

00:29:03.891 --> 00:29:06.150
And that is indeed what happened.

00:29:06.150 --> 00:29:11.040
So, next slide here shows

00:29:11.040 --> 00:29:13.890
the amount donated on the horizontal axis

00:29:13.890 --> 00:29:16.473
to what we call the principled option.

00:29:17.460 --> 00:29:19.895
That is the option that
was more consistent

00:29:19.895 --> 00:29:22.200
with democratic fair play.

00:29:22.200 --> 00:29:23.033
As you can see,

00:29:23.033 --> 00:29:25.655
Democrats when faced
between these two choices

00:29:25.655 --> 00:29:29.176
in the state of Missouri
were overwhelmingly donating

00:29:29.176 --> 00:29:32.769
most of their endowment
to the principled option,

00:29:32.769 --> 00:29:36.251
to the group that advocated
for non-partisan redistricting

00:29:36.251 --> 00:29:40.708
rather than one done by a partisan,

00:29:40.708 --> 00:29:43.469
in this case Republican
controlled legislature.

00:29:43.469 --> 00:29:46.560
It reveals, I think two
things, first of all,

00:29:46.560 --> 00:29:50.010
the Democrats in this scenario
knew very well where either

00:29:50.010 --> 00:29:51.120
democratic principles

00:29:51.120 --> 00:29:53.460
or their partisan partisan interests lie.

00:29:53.460 --> 00:29:56.356
They donated most of their
money to the principled option.

00:29:56.356 --> 00:29:59.570
But now, look what happens
when we asked Republicans

00:29:59.570 --> 00:30:03.120
to donate 10 cents, and
how they would donate it,

00:30:03.120 --> 00:30:05.649
remember this is the group
that here faces attention

00:30:05.649 --> 00:30:08.733
between their partisan interests
and democratic principles.

00:30:09.630 --> 00:30:12.411
You see that a majority
of Republicans in fact,

00:30:12.411 --> 00:30:16.685
decided to donate less
to the principled option

00:30:16.685 --> 00:30:18.690
to Clean Missouri,

00:30:18.690 --> 00:30:21.480
and more to the partisan option,

00:30:21.480 --> 00:30:25.380
to the group that advocating
returning redistricting powers

00:30:25.380 --> 00:30:28.140
to the Republican controlled legislature.

00:30:28.140 --> 00:30:31.110
The median donation was
only 3 cents out of 10

00:30:31.110 --> 00:30:33.150
to the principled option,

00:30:33.150 --> 00:30:35.880
and the mean donation was about 4 cents.

00:30:35.880 --> 00:30:39.960
In other words, the average individual

00:30:39.960 --> 00:30:42.780
donated a majority of her endowment

00:30:42.780 --> 00:30:45.960
to the partisan rather
than the principled option.

00:30:45.960 --> 00:30:50.960
And only 29% of subjects
of Republicans in this case

00:30:51.090 --> 00:30:54.081
donated to the option
that's more consistent

00:30:54.081 --> 00:30:56.640
with democratic principles.

00:30:56.640 --> 00:30:58.782
And this in effect highlights

00:30:58.782 --> 00:31:03.782
how I view the main
threats to US democracy.

00:31:06.196 --> 00:31:10.110
At one level, it is the fact
that this is even possible

00:31:10.110 --> 00:31:13.590
that voters in this state get to choose

00:31:13.590 --> 00:31:15.360
how redistricting is done.

00:31:15.360 --> 00:31:17.130
And I think, in the comparative context,

00:31:17.130 --> 00:31:19.974
the United States is
unique in the sense that

00:31:19.974 --> 00:31:24.123
state level institutions, legislatures,

00:31:25.260 --> 00:31:28.170
sometimes in conjunction with
the governor have the ability,

00:31:28.170 --> 00:31:31.969
and as a result the opportunity
to shape the fundamentals

00:31:31.969 --> 00:31:33.350
of political competition,

00:31:33.350 --> 00:31:36.166
including how redistricting
is done, who can vote,

00:31:36.166 --> 00:31:37.710
and so on.

00:31:37.710 --> 00:31:40.980
This is very unique around the world,

00:31:40.980 --> 00:31:43.935
and it simply creates an
institutional opportunity

00:31:43.935 --> 00:31:46.440
to rig the game in one's favor.

00:31:46.440 --> 00:31:48.210
The other condition that
I would like to highlight

00:31:48.210 --> 00:31:50.318
is in effect the shape of this histogram.

00:31:50.318 --> 00:31:55.084
That is that the vast
majority of the type of voter

00:31:55.084 --> 00:31:58.290
that in this scenario faced a trade-off

00:31:58.290 --> 00:32:02.610
between her partisan interests
and democratic principles

00:32:02.610 --> 00:32:05.940
decided to side with partisan interests.

00:32:05.940 --> 00:32:08.190
And that is what we find
throughout the study,

00:32:08.190 --> 00:32:10.710
both about Democrats and Republicans.

00:32:10.710 --> 00:32:15.057
And I see that as the other
main structural condition

00:32:15.057 --> 00:32:17.000
that represents a threat to democracy.

00:32:17.000 --> 00:32:20.640
And that is that most people
when facing a conflict

00:32:20.640 --> 00:32:23.250
between partisanship and
democratic principles

00:32:23.250 --> 00:32:25.710
are willing to side with the former.

00:32:25.710 --> 00:32:26.543
Thank you.

00:32:29.610 --> 00:32:32.910
- Great, thanks very much panelists.

00:32:32.910 --> 00:32:37.910
So, the panelists, I guess we
should join as a panel now.

00:32:40.140 --> 00:32:43.863
And just, I have a couple quick
questions to kick things off

00:32:43.863 --> 00:32:48.840
and then we can turn to
questions from the audience.

00:32:48.840 --> 00:32:52.293
So, I wanna just ask Sue,
just a clarification question.

00:32:52.293 --> 00:32:54.431
Regarding income inequality,

00:32:54.431 --> 00:32:58.381
do you find that the income
inequality relationship

00:32:58.381 --> 00:33:01.879
between income inequality
and democratic erosion,

00:33:01.879 --> 00:33:04.530
does that hold for high-income countries

00:33:04.530 --> 00:33:06.686
as well as low income countries?

00:33:06.686 --> 00:33:11.373
So, right. Now, that's the question.

00:33:14.940 --> 00:33:19.940
- So, if you interact in,
I let this one answer,

00:33:20.644 --> 00:33:23.160
I don't think it's the answer
to the question you're asking,

00:33:23.160 --> 00:33:26.550
but we also look at GDP
per capita separately,

00:33:26.550 --> 00:33:29.026
you know, as another
factor and find that it-

00:33:29.026 --> 00:33:30.660
- Question about interaction,

00:33:30.660 --> 00:33:31.590
yeah.
- Right.

00:33:31.590 --> 00:33:34.348
I don't remember.

00:33:34.348 --> 00:33:36.366
So, my co-author on that paper

00:33:36.366 --> 00:33:40.200
is another recent Yale grad named Eli Rau.

00:33:40.200 --> 00:33:43.460
Yale is full of amazing
graduate students who...

00:33:44.400 --> 00:33:47.649
And I don't remember, I'm gonna find out.

00:33:47.649 --> 00:33:48.914
But income itself

00:33:48.914 --> 00:33:53.914
has a sort of suggestive
but unstable effect.

00:33:55.669 --> 00:33:59.850
You know, the genie effect is just like,

00:33:59.850 --> 00:34:01.860
we couldn't get rid of
it no matter what we did,

00:34:01.860 --> 00:34:04.140
you know, it was sort of
however you measure it,

00:34:04.140 --> 00:34:06.840
however you measure
the dependent variable,

00:34:06.840 --> 00:34:09.315
you know, the only way we got rid of it

00:34:09.315 --> 00:34:11.365
was like unplugging our computers.

00:34:11.365 --> 00:34:15.276
So, it was pretty remarkable.

00:34:15.276 --> 00:34:17.280
- Have you tried kicking the computer?

00:34:17.280 --> 00:34:18.113
Yeah.
- I do. (laughs)

00:34:18.113 --> 00:34:19.476
- Okay, so, and then

00:34:19.476 --> 00:34:20.734
my second question,

00:34:20.734 --> 00:34:23.043
and this is really for all the panelists,

00:34:26.430 --> 00:34:28.860
this is obviously the
idea of democratic erosion

00:34:28.860 --> 00:34:31.460
in the United States is a
very frightening prospect.

00:34:32.580 --> 00:34:35.700
And at one level, it's obviously,

00:34:35.700 --> 00:34:37.230
I'll just say with it's very frightening.

00:34:37.230 --> 00:34:40.004
On the other level, you know,
you wonder whether or not

00:34:40.004 --> 00:34:42.174
this is a bunch of political scientists

00:34:42.174 --> 00:34:45.941
engaging in catastrophizing,

00:34:45.941 --> 00:34:49.710
and whether this is a real thing or not.

00:34:49.710 --> 00:34:53.435
And so, I mean, each of you or any of you,

00:34:53.435 --> 00:34:58.435
could you spell out what
you think of as pathways

00:34:58.950 --> 00:35:03.531
that you think are plausible,
maybe, one or two scenarios

00:35:03.531 --> 00:35:07.793
that you can think of that would
produce what you've defined

00:35:07.793 --> 00:35:10.750
as democratic erosion?

00:35:10.750 --> 00:35:11.897
I'm using Sue's term,

00:35:11.897 --> 00:35:14.381
but democratic backsliding,

00:35:14.381 --> 00:35:19.293
I think that's what Milan
was referring to it as.

00:35:20.280 --> 00:35:22.650
And then, you know, just a
little bit about the plausibility

00:35:22.650 --> 00:35:25.743
in your view of those scenarios.

00:35:30.660 --> 00:35:33.120
Anyone can just start. Yeah.

00:35:33.120 --> 00:35:35.233
- Sure, I can jump in real quick.

00:35:35.233 --> 00:35:40.233
So, I share that general
orientation that often

00:35:41.850 --> 00:35:45.540
I fear that, you know, that
we who are focused so much

00:35:45.540 --> 00:35:48.125
on politics can get
ourselves much more upset

00:35:48.125 --> 00:35:51.180
than those who are casual observers.

00:35:51.180 --> 00:35:52.501
But I think in this case,

00:35:52.501 --> 00:35:57.501
if anything I feel like we're
understating some of the risks

00:35:57.689 --> 00:36:01.440
and I would say there are two pathways.

00:36:01.440 --> 00:36:02.490
So, the first thing to say is,

00:36:02.490 --> 00:36:05.010
I think this is very consistent
with what Sue was saying

00:36:05.010 --> 00:36:10.010
is that your concern is
erosion, not collapse, right?

00:36:11.440 --> 00:36:13.770
The way democracies tend to end,

00:36:13.770 --> 00:36:15.120
you know, and a whimper,

00:36:15.120 --> 00:36:16.808
not a bang in the contemporary

00:36:16.808 --> 00:36:21.808
as Ziblatta and Levitsky
have shown in their book.

00:36:22.290 --> 00:36:24.913
And so, what you're really
worried about is this sort

00:36:24.913 --> 00:36:27.120
of self-reinforcing process.

00:36:27.120 --> 00:36:29.940
And I think both of us are arguing

00:36:29.940 --> 00:36:32.010
that inequality polarization,

00:36:32.010 --> 00:36:34.620
and the distinctive character
of American institutions,

00:36:34.620 --> 00:36:36.150
I would say all three of us are,

00:36:36.150 --> 00:36:38.130
but certainly Sue and I
were emphasizing this,

00:36:38.130 --> 00:36:40.020
have created a kind of more,

00:36:40.020 --> 00:36:41.580
what I call a vicious cycle, right?

00:36:41.580 --> 00:36:44.771
Where there is real possibility
of self-reinforcing,

00:36:44.771 --> 00:36:47.343
erosion of democratic institutions.

00:36:48.300 --> 00:36:50.989
And I don't discount the possibility

00:36:50.989 --> 00:36:55.479
that there would be a more
like dramatic single event.

00:36:55.479 --> 00:36:57.990
And you know, there's a lot of concern

00:36:57.990 --> 00:36:59.770
and I share that concern about

00:37:00.894 --> 00:37:05.894
authoritarian minded president
bent on revenge (chuckles)

00:37:06.452 --> 00:37:08.178
against political enemies,

00:37:08.178 --> 00:37:10.194
and much more savvy about
how to use the institutions

00:37:10.194 --> 00:37:13.369
of American government
could well get a lot farther

00:37:13.369 --> 00:37:17.670
than said president did while in office.

00:37:17.670 --> 00:37:20.670
But I wanted to highlight
that I think the trends

00:37:20.670 --> 00:37:24.210
that we've seen that are really
linked to this other form

00:37:24.210 --> 00:37:26.149
of democratic erosion too,

00:37:26.149 --> 00:37:30.634
namely the sort of creeping
humanitarianism that Milan

00:37:30.634 --> 00:37:35.440
talked about in that the
supporting of policies that

00:37:38.430 --> 00:37:41.640
and institutional changes
that sort of chip away

00:37:41.640 --> 00:37:44.226
at the the hallmarks of democracy.

00:37:44.226 --> 00:37:48.815
And that, I think because it
builds on some of the features

00:37:48.815 --> 00:37:52.260
of our political institutions
that can be exploited

00:37:52.260 --> 00:37:55.500
for partisan gain is in some
ways a kind of more insidious

00:37:55.500 --> 00:37:58.380
and difficult to counter form of erosion.

00:37:58.380 --> 00:38:03.380
And so, you know, I don't know
if people a generation ago

00:38:04.735 --> 00:38:08.014
would've expected the level
of really extreme partisan

00:38:08.014 --> 00:38:10.890
gerrymandering that's taken place

00:38:10.890 --> 00:38:12.663
and been sanctioned by the court.

00:38:13.530 --> 00:38:16.110
Certainly, before the contemporary

00:38:16.110 --> 00:38:17.850
urban rural split occurred,

00:38:17.850 --> 00:38:21.480
there wasn't nearly as much
of a bias in the institutions

00:38:21.480 --> 00:38:26.480
that are now portioned in
favor of less urban places

00:38:27.725 --> 00:38:29.973
as we have today.

00:38:30.840 --> 00:38:32.730
Or you know, the kinds of challenges

00:38:32.730 --> 00:38:35.910
that have taken place at the
state level to election result.

00:38:35.910 --> 00:38:38.490
All those seem to be
pretty fundamental shifts.

00:38:38.490 --> 00:38:40.530
And then, if we step beyond
kind of institutions,

00:38:40.530 --> 00:38:42.600
I mentioned, and I really
wanna emphasize like,

00:38:42.600 --> 00:38:45.927
the kind of claims against
democracy that are becoming

00:38:45.927 --> 00:38:50.310
and against partisan
opponents that are being used

00:38:50.310 --> 00:38:53.400
in extreme media,
particularly on the right,

00:38:53.400 --> 00:38:54.660
but on both the right and the left,

00:38:54.660 --> 00:38:56.910
I think it's also a
really significant erosion

00:38:56.910 --> 00:39:00.150
of core aspects of our
democratic heritage.

00:39:00.150 --> 00:39:01.463
So, that, to me, is-

00:39:01.463 --> 00:39:02.621
- Thank you.
- Yeah, that's a long

00:39:02.621 --> 00:39:03.631
answer to a short question.

00:39:03.631 --> 00:39:07.500
But I don't wanna say we
should all be freaked out.

00:39:07.500 --> 00:39:09.180
It's not like in that scene in a airplane,

00:39:09.180 --> 00:39:11.700
if you remember that '80s funny movie

00:39:11.700 --> 00:39:14.010
where like there's a sign
that said don't panic

00:39:14.010 --> 00:39:15.360
when the plane started going down,

00:39:15.360 --> 00:39:17.370
and then, at one point,
it just shift to panic

00:39:17.370 --> 00:39:19.440
and everyone jumped up
and started screaming.

00:39:19.440 --> 00:39:20.610
I'm not saying panic,

00:39:20.610 --> 00:39:21.756
but I'm saying be concerned

00:39:21.756 --> 00:39:25.709
and be focused on what
are the real sources

00:39:25.709 --> 00:39:27.250
of this erosion.

00:39:27.250 --> 00:39:28.702
- Good, Good. Thank you, Jacob.

00:39:28.702 --> 00:39:32.162
Sue, what what are some
of the scenarios that

00:39:32.162 --> 00:39:37.162
in the US context you
think of as worrisome?

00:39:38.400 --> 00:39:41.220
And then, Milan is now back.

00:39:41.220 --> 00:39:44.130
- So, one of the things that I
worry about in the US context

00:39:44.130 --> 00:39:49.130
is the unshakeability of
Donald Trump's electoral base.

00:39:51.030 --> 00:39:53.812
And it's sort of seeming
kind of insularity

00:39:53.812 --> 00:39:56.666
from any kind of, you
know, negative information

00:39:56.666 --> 00:39:59.070
or negative facts about the candidate,

00:39:59.070 --> 00:40:01.633
about the party, about the policies.

00:40:01.633 --> 00:40:04.070
When you look at other countries

00:40:04.070 --> 00:40:08.877
that have had periods
of erosion and kind of,

00:40:08.877 --> 00:40:10.980
and kind of set them aside,

00:40:10.980 --> 00:40:12.990
one of the things that you see is that,

00:40:12.990 --> 00:40:14.760
you know, parties lose elections,

00:40:14.760 --> 00:40:17.409
and eroding parties lose elections,

00:40:17.409 --> 00:40:19.410
and they really kind of,

00:40:19.410 --> 00:40:22.923
it's possible to undo
some of what happened,

00:40:24.336 --> 00:40:26.430
you know, and you don't have the same,

00:40:26.430 --> 00:40:29.228
sometimes you have
challenging of the election.

00:40:29.228 --> 00:40:33.503
So, Jair Bolsonaro in Brazil
was very much like Trump

00:40:33.503 --> 00:40:35.280
in that he claimed before the election

00:40:35.280 --> 00:40:36.630
that there was gonna be massive fraud,

00:40:36.630 --> 00:40:38.550
but he didn't really
double-down on that claim

00:40:38.550 --> 00:40:40.410
afterwards so much and that's probably

00:40:40.410 --> 00:40:42.840
because he was really
worried about prosecution,

00:40:42.840 --> 00:40:44.220
and he is being prosecuted.

00:40:44.220 --> 00:40:49.220
So, but you know, the UK is
an interesting experience

00:40:50.820 --> 00:40:53.610
because they really did
have a very close call,

00:40:53.610 --> 00:40:55.703
maybe closer call than
we realized in the US.

00:40:55.703 --> 00:40:57.502
So, the proration crisis

00:40:57.502 --> 00:41:02.502
where Prime Minister Johnson
closed down the parliament,

00:41:05.850 --> 00:41:07.740
which is not something that never happens,

00:41:07.740 --> 00:41:11.943
but it was found that he had
done that in a unlawful manner.

00:41:12.990 --> 00:41:14.718
I'm not sure what
unlawful and illegal are,

00:41:14.718 --> 00:41:16.140
the difference between them,

00:41:16.140 --> 00:41:18.960
but that's what the
court decided in the UK.

00:41:18.960 --> 00:41:20.593
And so, that's kind of a big thing.

00:41:20.593 --> 00:41:24.068
And then, they also have
engaged in you know,

00:41:24.068 --> 00:41:27.153
restrictions to ballot access.

00:41:28.980 --> 00:41:30.540
You know, like the US a country

00:41:30.540 --> 00:41:32.280
in which there's very
little electoral fraud.

00:41:32.280 --> 00:41:34.823
In fact, the elections are much more

00:41:34.823 --> 00:41:37.049
heavily regulated there.

00:41:37.049 --> 00:41:40.343
And the right to protest
has been curtailed in ways

00:41:40.343 --> 00:41:42.620
that have been lasting.

00:41:42.620 --> 00:41:44.460
In fact, we saw a lot of drama around that

00:41:44.460 --> 00:41:47.280
the last few days.

00:41:47.280 --> 00:41:50.285
But what happened to Johnson
was that he was dumped

00:41:50.285 --> 00:41:51.386
by his political party.

00:41:51.386 --> 00:41:53.375
And he was dumped by his political party.

00:41:53.375 --> 00:41:55.950
And granted it's a, you
know, parliamentary system,

00:41:55.950 --> 00:41:58.620
it's easier for the party to
get rid of his prime minister.

00:41:58.620 --> 00:41:59.880
You don't need a new election,

00:41:59.880 --> 00:42:02.430
you don't even need a
party election there.

00:42:02.430 --> 00:42:05.310
But the reason he was dumped
was that he was very unpopular

00:42:05.310 --> 00:42:06.826
or to put it the other way around,

00:42:06.826 --> 00:42:09.556
had he had the persistent
kind of level of popularity

00:42:09.556 --> 00:42:11.879
that Donald Trump has in the US,

00:42:11.879 --> 00:42:13.770
they might well have hung onto him,

00:42:13.770 --> 00:42:17.610
and then we might have seen
more of an unraveling in the UK.

00:42:17.610 --> 00:42:19.923
So, that's a real source
of concern for me.

00:42:22.500 --> 00:42:23.527
- Great, thank you.

00:42:23.527 --> 00:42:25.923
Milan, do you have
anything you wanted to add?

00:42:26.850 --> 00:42:29.337
- I apologize, I just
appeared for a minute there.

00:42:29.337 --> 00:42:32.009
And was it the question
about what are the?

00:42:32.009 --> 00:42:34.080
- Yeah, the question is
are there any scenarios

00:42:34.080 --> 00:42:37.230
that you might, that
you know, that just...

00:42:37.230 --> 00:42:38.580
Basically, are there any scenarios

00:42:38.580 --> 00:42:40.650
that you are especially worried about?

00:42:40.650 --> 00:42:42.190
You've mentioned state level

00:42:43.680 --> 00:42:46.770
machinations in your presentation.

00:42:46.770 --> 00:42:48.994
Is that what you're
concerned about mostly?

00:42:48.994 --> 00:42:51.450
Or what are the things you're
concerned about in terms

00:42:51.450 --> 00:42:54.270
of scenarios for democratic backsliding?

00:42:54.270 --> 00:42:58.500
- So, those state level, you know,

00:42:58.500 --> 00:43:01.177
disenfranchisement, gerrymandering

00:43:01.177 --> 00:43:04.620
that was happening before
the Trump presidency,

00:43:04.620 --> 00:43:06.180
it's happening now.

00:43:06.180 --> 00:43:11.180
And those are the
institutional opportunities

00:43:11.400 --> 00:43:13.410
that are unique to the United States

00:43:13.410 --> 00:43:17.379
and that are being taken
advantage of by both parties.

00:43:17.379 --> 00:43:19.230
And I think that's very concerning.

00:43:19.230 --> 00:43:21.364
And maybe, let me add two other elements

00:43:21.364 --> 00:43:23.463
that I think are structurally different,

00:43:25.320 --> 00:43:27.480
let's say, from what was
happening four years ago

00:43:27.480 --> 00:43:28.380
or six years ago.

00:43:28.380 --> 00:43:31.084
And the first one is,
I think, that similar

00:43:31.084 --> 00:43:33.210
to what Sue mentioned,

00:43:33.210 --> 00:43:37.380
that post-January 6th
republicans are different.

00:43:37.380 --> 00:43:40.440
And maybe, one way I would
describe this in concrete terms

00:43:40.440 --> 00:43:44.280
that we see it in the study
that I briefly previewed.

00:43:44.280 --> 00:43:46.967
As I mentioned, we conducted
two studies, one in 2020,

00:43:46.967 --> 00:43:49.830
just before the 2020 election,

00:43:49.830 --> 00:43:53.070
and another one in the 2022 election.

00:43:53.070 --> 00:43:56.467
And what we saw in the
study conducted just prior

00:43:56.467 --> 00:43:59.421
to the midterms that is
after January 6th, 2021,

00:43:59.421 --> 00:44:03.250
is that we saw that republicans

00:44:05.100 --> 00:44:07.080
are unwilling to punish.

00:44:07.080 --> 00:44:08.700
So, whereas in 2020 they were,

00:44:08.700 --> 00:44:11.610
but in 2022, they were unwilling to punish

00:44:11.610 --> 00:44:14.820
one type of undemocratic behavior,

00:44:14.820 --> 00:44:17.850
and that is denying the
outcome of elections,

00:44:17.850 --> 00:44:22.380
questioning the fairness or
the conduct of elections.

00:44:22.380 --> 00:44:24.090
When their candidate was claiming,

00:44:24.090 --> 00:44:26.160
and these were real world
candidates that were,

00:44:26.160 --> 00:44:27.750
simply questioning the results

00:44:27.750 --> 00:44:30.750
of the 2020 presidential
elections or some other elections,

00:44:30.750 --> 00:44:32.700
Republican voters were
willing to go along with it

00:44:32.700 --> 00:44:35.310
in some cases that carried a premium.

00:44:35.310 --> 00:44:37.380
And so, that's very different

00:44:37.380 --> 00:44:39.090
from the kind of partisan asymmetry

00:44:39.090 --> 00:44:40.920
that we have observed before.

00:44:40.920 --> 00:44:43.380
But actually, voters on
both sides were willing

00:44:43.380 --> 00:44:47.073
to overlook democratic
defects on their own side.

00:44:48.150 --> 00:44:49.440
This is very different now.

00:44:49.440 --> 00:44:52.784
And maybe, the last comment
I would have is that

00:44:52.784 --> 00:44:56.730
another feature that is different
from just a few years ago

00:44:56.730 --> 00:44:59.669
is that both sides are
increasingly willing

00:44:59.669 --> 00:45:04.669
to justify their own undemocratic behavior

00:45:04.800 --> 00:45:08.730
by pointing to the other
sides on democratic behavior.

00:45:08.730 --> 00:45:12.503
So, to maybe, give one concrete example,

00:45:12.503 --> 00:45:17.503
the Democrats when they
redistricted the state of New York

00:45:17.680 --> 00:45:22.260
just before the 2022 midterms,

00:45:22.260 --> 00:45:25.326
that redistricting was ruled
as unconstitutional in New York

00:45:25.326 --> 00:45:27.780
because it was gerrymandered.

00:45:27.780 --> 00:45:29.838
But the rationale that was given is that

00:45:29.838 --> 00:45:32.154
we have to do this because Republicans

00:45:32.154 --> 00:45:34.663
are doing this elsewhere.

00:45:34.663 --> 00:45:36.368
And if we don't do this,

00:45:36.368 --> 00:45:40.020
we'll be hurting ourselves politically.

00:45:40.020 --> 00:45:43.303
And if you look at some
of the commentary on this,

00:45:43.303 --> 00:45:45.270
and reporting what was interesting,

00:45:45.270 --> 00:45:48.147
that is the journalist
sometimes noted and you know,

00:45:48.147 --> 00:45:50.160
and no voter is going to punish

00:45:50.160 --> 00:45:51.840
their own party for gerrymandering.

00:45:51.840 --> 00:45:53.940
So, there's no political cost to this.

00:45:53.940 --> 00:45:55.590
And I think this kind of tit for tat

00:45:55.590 --> 00:45:58.973
where one side is saying
we have to play dirty

00:45:58.973 --> 00:46:01.795
because the other side
is already doing it,

00:46:01.795 --> 00:46:04.353
can kind of spiral out of control.

00:46:05.700 --> 00:46:09.813
- Okay, well, that is a really
a great way to transition

00:46:09.813 --> 00:46:11.820
to the questions

00:46:11.820 --> 00:46:15.660
'cause I find that really
(chuckles) disturbing,

00:46:15.660 --> 00:46:19.881
I mean, the idea that sort
of norm violation leads

00:46:19.881 --> 00:46:24.120
to a kind of spiral of escalation,

00:46:24.120 --> 00:46:26.111
you know, of norm violation.

00:46:26.111 --> 00:46:28.350
That is, I would just say,

00:46:28.350 --> 00:46:30.630
I'm sorry, maybe I should stop here.

00:46:30.630 --> 00:46:33.469
But I would say the scenarios
that seem most disturbing

00:46:33.469 --> 00:46:37.530
to me for extreme problems
have that feature to them

00:46:37.530 --> 00:46:40.980
where people start down
on a set of things,

00:46:40.980 --> 00:46:43.094
they start down a path,

00:46:43.094 --> 00:46:45.240
which through a series of,

00:46:45.240 --> 00:46:47.310
I have to do this because they did this,

00:46:47.310 --> 00:46:49.164
or if I don't do it, they'll do it.

00:46:49.164 --> 00:46:52.856
That logic can lead you
very far down the road

00:46:52.856 --> 00:46:54.409
in a scary direction.

00:46:54.409 --> 00:46:55.860
And anyway, on that note,

00:46:55.860 --> 00:47:00.093
why don't we turn to our audience.

00:47:01.860 --> 00:47:06.559
Rick Harrison has synthesized
some of the questions.

00:47:06.559 --> 00:47:10.350
And so, I'm gonna read
some of them to you.

00:47:10.350 --> 00:47:12.350
And let me just go ahead.

00:47:12.350 --> 00:47:15.059
And anyone who wants, I
don't think any of these,

00:47:15.059 --> 00:47:17.339
at least the first bunch are not directed

00:47:17.339 --> 00:47:19.260
to any particular panelists.

00:47:19.260 --> 00:47:22.179
So, whoever thinks they
have something insightful

00:47:22.179 --> 00:47:25.849
to say should please share
that with our audience.

00:47:25.849 --> 00:47:28.950
Okay, here's a question.

00:47:28.950 --> 00:47:30.069
Peter Turchin has written that,

00:47:30.069 --> 00:47:33.662
"We are in for a period
of ongoing instability

00:47:33.662 --> 00:47:37.470
because of four underlying
societal forces,

00:47:37.470 --> 00:47:40.560
popular commiseration
leading to mass mobilization,

00:47:40.560 --> 00:47:43.649
potential, elite overproduction,"

00:47:43.649 --> 00:47:46.605
which that's his famous hypothesis about

00:47:46.605 --> 00:47:48.636
elite overproduction,

00:47:48.636 --> 00:47:52.271
"failing fiscal health and
weakened legitimacy of the state

00:47:52.271 --> 00:47:54.900
and geopolitical factors.

00:47:54.900 --> 00:47:56.940
And so, some of those
things were mentioned by,

00:47:56.940 --> 00:47:59.700
I guess, Jacob mentioned at and as well,

00:47:59.700 --> 00:48:01.803
Susan, and also, I guess, Milan as well.

00:48:02.970 --> 00:48:07.110
What do you think about the
implications of this thesis?

00:48:07.110 --> 00:48:08.520
What do you feel about the thesis?

00:48:08.520 --> 00:48:10.499
Do you find this a useful,

00:48:10.499 --> 00:48:11.550
this a lead over product,

00:48:11.550 --> 00:48:15.870
especially a lead over production
and legitimacy problems?

00:48:15.870 --> 00:48:17.640
Do you find that a useful framework?

00:48:17.640 --> 00:48:20.703
And how does that help you
think about these questions?

00:48:26.280 --> 00:48:27.827
I'm very interested in
hearing what you think about

00:48:27.827 --> 00:48:29.268
a lead over production.

00:48:29.268 --> 00:48:30.674
But anything in this question

00:48:30.674 --> 00:48:33.480
I'd be interested in
hearing your reaction to.

00:48:33.480 --> 00:48:36.071
Are you familiar with this
elite over production thesis?

00:48:36.071 --> 00:48:38.110
Okay, we don't have
enough time to go into it.

00:48:38.110 --> 00:48:42.630
But basically, if I've got it right,

00:48:42.630 --> 00:48:45.150
when you produce more political elites

00:48:45.150 --> 00:48:47.544
than you have places for them in society,

00:48:47.544 --> 00:48:52.350
that's the tinder for political conflict

00:48:52.350 --> 00:48:55.290
and potential major changes
in the political system.

00:48:55.290 --> 00:49:00.290
And this, I think, goes
back to, is it Pareto?

00:49:00.423 --> 00:49:04.811
Who talked about elite
fractures being the foundation

00:49:04.811 --> 00:49:08.895
for a political change as opposed

00:49:08.895 --> 00:49:12.480
to mass based political change.

00:49:12.480 --> 00:49:13.313
Obviously, you know,

00:49:13.313 --> 00:49:15.990
what a great comparative
politics scholar I am.

00:49:15.990 --> 00:49:20.133
So, any (chuckles) comments
from our colleagues on that?

00:49:22.428 --> 00:49:25.170
I'm tempted to say we're
gonna edit this part out

00:49:25.170 --> 00:49:26.214
of the video.

00:49:26.214 --> 00:49:29.073
Okay, next question.

00:49:30.360 --> 00:49:32.460
So, any comments please? Yeah.

00:49:32.460 --> 00:49:33.690
- I don't know if I have anything

00:49:33.690 --> 00:49:35.274
to say on elite over production,

00:49:35.274 --> 00:49:40.274
but I will say that the
role I think both Sue

00:49:40.875 --> 00:49:44.573
and I were emphasizing the
degree to which the concentration

00:49:44.573 --> 00:49:48.090
of economic rewards is
quite consequential.

00:49:48.090 --> 00:49:49.443
That's one kind of elite.

00:49:50.370 --> 00:49:53.505
Actually, I think, probably
one of the big problems

00:49:53.505 --> 00:49:56.730
in our politics today is this
linked to what you were saying

00:49:56.730 --> 00:49:59.520
about norm erosion is the
people who go into office

00:49:59.520 --> 00:50:01.608
increasingly have to be
partisan warriors, right?

00:50:01.608 --> 00:50:03.474
And so, you know,

00:50:03.474 --> 00:50:05.558
I think we really can't understate

00:50:05.558 --> 00:50:07.533
the role of elites in this process.

00:50:07.533 --> 00:50:12.533
So, in both shaping how
non-elites are thinking,

00:50:13.260 --> 00:50:16.503
but also, in turn their own struggles.

00:50:16.503 --> 00:50:19.098
And so, one thing that
I think is quite clear

00:50:19.098 --> 00:50:20.576
is that the pool of people

00:50:20.576 --> 00:50:23.043
who are going into public office now

00:50:23.043 --> 00:50:26.380
is very much shaped by both this very

00:50:28.470 --> 00:50:31.710
bifurcated media ecosystem
that I alluded to.

00:50:31.710 --> 00:50:32.795
And by the sense,

00:50:32.795 --> 00:50:35.940
that there's a struggle
that's existential struggle

00:50:35.940 --> 00:50:37.754
that each party is engaged in.

00:50:37.754 --> 00:50:39.854
And I do think there are differences

00:50:39.854 --> 00:50:41.700
between the parties in this regard.

00:50:41.700 --> 00:50:44.028
And I've been studying the
Democratic party a lot lately

00:50:44.028 --> 00:50:46.410
'cause I was studying the Republican party

00:50:46.410 --> 00:50:47.689
for so much in my career.

00:50:47.689 --> 00:50:49.779
And I do think one thing that is different

00:50:49.779 --> 00:50:53.400
is that the Democratic party
still has a lot of people who,

00:50:53.400 --> 00:50:54.810
for one of a better way of putting it,

00:50:54.810 --> 00:50:56.460
kind of wanna do policy.

00:50:56.460 --> 00:50:59.460
And that has a kind of cohesive,

00:50:59.460 --> 00:51:01.980
it induces a kind of
cohesion across the party.

00:51:01.980 --> 00:51:04.410
If you look at the
Republicans say in the house,

00:51:04.410 --> 00:51:06.200
one of their big problems, right?

00:51:06.200 --> 00:51:10.175
Is that they lack this kind
of common denominator of,

00:51:10.175 --> 00:51:12.658
well, we just wanna like do policy things

00:51:12.658 --> 00:51:14.202
through government, right?

00:51:14.202 --> 00:51:18.553
There's sort of one faction
that's trying to win favor on

00:51:22.230 --> 00:51:23.790
conservative social media.

00:51:23.790 --> 00:51:25.820
There's other factions that
would like to do policy things.

00:51:25.820 --> 00:51:27.330
There's others that would think

00:51:27.330 --> 00:51:29.267
the Supreme Court's
really all that matters.

00:51:29.267 --> 00:51:32.353
You know, and so, I
think creates a much more

00:51:32.353 --> 00:51:33.750
fractured party

00:51:33.750 --> 00:51:35.940
and I think fractures kind of bad.

00:51:35.940 --> 00:51:38.693
Like, I think that in
general you want parties

00:51:38.693 --> 00:51:41.068
that can behave a bit more cohesively

00:51:41.068 --> 00:51:44.160
and will discipline of
course their members

00:51:44.160 --> 00:51:45.908
when they're behaving in ways

00:51:45.908 --> 00:51:48.604
that sort of reflect poorly
on the party overall.

00:51:48.604 --> 00:51:53.604
And so, not to to paint
the Democrats as being

00:51:55.410 --> 00:51:56.819
really high performers in this regard.

00:51:56.819 --> 00:51:59.280
But I do think one thing that
we should be thinking about

00:51:59.280 --> 00:52:03.030
is like what, you know, how
does a Republican party,

00:52:03.030 --> 00:52:05.957
because our system does not
work without two parties

00:52:05.957 --> 00:52:08.580
that are relatively well functioning.

00:52:08.580 --> 00:52:10.190
How do we get the
Republican party to function

00:52:10.190 --> 00:52:14.130
as a more cohesive party,
and not just in the way

00:52:14.130 --> 00:52:17.700
that Milan suggest they
might function as a party.

00:52:17.700 --> 00:52:20.760
So, I'm sorry that I was
exactly on elite overproduction,

00:52:20.760 --> 00:52:23.340
but I actually think it's really important

00:52:23.340 --> 00:52:26.430
to think about the elites
in American politics,

00:52:26.430 --> 00:52:30.750
and how they play into this process.

00:52:30.750 --> 00:52:32.051
- Okay, Sue, Milan,

00:52:32.051 --> 00:52:33.660
do you have anything you wanna say?

00:52:33.660 --> 00:52:36.060
Or like, we should move
on to the next question?

00:52:37.860 --> 00:52:38.970
- Sure, next question sounds great.

00:52:38.970 --> 00:52:40.203
- Okay, next question.

00:52:43.050 --> 00:52:48.018
Do you think that the
candidate selection mechanism?

00:52:48.018 --> 00:52:50.850
So, we've got the situation
in the United States

00:52:50.850 --> 00:52:52.290
where we have Donald Trump

00:52:52.290 --> 00:52:56.266
and Joe Biden likely to be the
next presidential candidates.

00:52:56.266 --> 00:52:58.356
That's a function to a great extent

00:52:58.356 --> 00:53:02.780
of the political institutions
for selecting office holders.

00:53:02.780 --> 00:53:05.430
Is there anything in your
research that suggests

00:53:05.430 --> 00:53:07.680
that they're better and worse ways

00:53:07.680 --> 00:53:10.330
in terms of the political institutions

00:53:13.380 --> 00:53:16.410
that might help us to
avoid democratic erosion?

00:53:16.410 --> 00:53:18.548
So, just to amplify on this a little bit,

00:53:18.548 --> 00:53:22.290
you suggested these deep
structural economic factors,

00:53:22.290 --> 00:53:24.000
attitudinal factors.

00:53:24.000 --> 00:53:27.835
Are there any institutional
factors that might be helpful?

00:53:27.835 --> 00:53:29.220
I mean, Milan of course,

00:53:29.220 --> 00:53:33.690
discussed the state level
institutional authority.

00:53:33.690 --> 00:53:36.146
But sue any of your
comparative politics work

00:53:36.146 --> 00:53:38.320
that might give us ideas about better

00:53:38.320 --> 00:53:41.640
and worse political institutions?

00:53:41.640 --> 00:53:44.253
Or Milan, anything that you would suggest?

00:53:48.690 --> 00:53:53.160
- So, you know, I think that there are,

00:53:53.160 --> 00:53:55.650
it's commonly viewed that
there are aspects of the way

00:53:55.650 --> 00:54:00.166
that we select candidates and
elect office holders in the US

00:54:00.166 --> 00:54:01.766
that contribute to polarization,

00:54:01.766 --> 00:54:06.766
and to party, and discipline
that Jacob was just discussing.

00:54:08.250 --> 00:54:10.455
And there are some kind of
structural aspects of that.

00:54:10.455 --> 00:54:13.140
So, the United States has gone through

00:54:13.140 --> 00:54:14.400
sometimes called the great sort

00:54:14.400 --> 00:54:15.960
where we have an increasing

00:54:15.960 --> 00:54:18.690
kind of homogenization regionally,

00:54:18.690 --> 00:54:23.690
and I electoral district of
in, so, less heterogeneity

00:54:24.510 --> 00:54:27.496
in terms of political
preferences and other factors.

00:54:27.496 --> 00:54:31.502
So, that tends to produce representatives

00:54:31.502 --> 00:54:36.502
who are not vulnerable to losing elections

00:54:36.719 --> 00:54:38.289
or to the other party,

00:54:38.289 --> 00:54:39.891
but rather that the dynamic

00:54:39.891 --> 00:54:42.150
tends to be within their own party.

00:54:42.150 --> 00:54:46.246
And that that can contribute
to the kind of polarization

00:54:46.246 --> 00:54:48.600
that Milan was talking about.

00:54:48.600 --> 00:54:49.890
There actually has been,

00:54:49.890 --> 00:54:51.150
I mean, there are trade-offs involved

00:54:51.150 --> 00:54:53.850
in all of these kinds of
institutional factors.

00:54:53.850 --> 00:54:58.850
So, in Latin America where I
have more regional expertise,

00:54:58.950 --> 00:55:02.340
there actually has been a
shift toward party primaries,

00:55:02.340 --> 00:55:04.950
not so much for metaverse of legislature

00:55:04.950 --> 00:55:07.590
who tend to be elected in
proportional representation

00:55:07.590 --> 00:55:08.700
on party lists,

00:55:08.700 --> 00:55:13.080
but for executives, for presidents.

00:55:13.080 --> 00:55:16.470
And the reason parties
like to do that is that

00:55:16.470 --> 00:55:20.476
it opens the process and
potentially chooses more popular

00:55:20.476 --> 00:55:23.280
candidates who will do better
in the general election.

00:55:23.280 --> 00:55:26.070
And also, I think, gives
voters a sense of ownership

00:55:26.070 --> 00:55:28.189
having participated in an open primary,

00:55:28.189 --> 00:55:31.038
and decided, well, I'm
gonna vote it in the PAN

00:55:31.038 --> 00:55:34.380
primary in Mexico, I'm
more likely then to go on

00:55:34.380 --> 00:55:37.407
and vote for the pun
candidate in the election.

00:55:37.407 --> 00:55:38.883
So, there are trade-offs.

00:55:41.610 --> 00:55:45.531
But there certainly are
some particular features

00:55:45.531 --> 00:55:48.351
of American institutions,
which Jacob was talking about

00:55:48.351 --> 00:55:53.351
as well as Milan, that tend
to increase polarization.

00:55:54.210 --> 00:55:55.454
I'm just gonna add one other thing.

00:55:55.454 --> 00:55:57.240
This is kind of adding to the mix,

00:55:57.240 --> 00:56:01.773
the super interesting
conversation about polarization.

00:56:02.730 --> 00:56:05.988
So, one of the drawbacks of
polarization as a strategy

00:56:05.988 --> 00:56:10.423
of democratic odors is that
it mobilizes your base,

00:56:10.423 --> 00:56:12.450
but it also mobilizes the other guys.

00:56:12.450 --> 00:56:14.794
So, you know, Donald
Trump says the Democrats

00:56:14.794 --> 00:56:18.287
are an angry mob who
want to open the border,

00:56:18.287 --> 00:56:19.120
and so on, and so forth.

00:56:19.120 --> 00:56:21.510
And you know, that gets
his folks revved up,

00:56:21.510 --> 00:56:23.580
and it also tends to
get Democrats revved up.

00:56:23.580 --> 00:56:26.168
So, there's a another strategy

00:56:26.168 --> 00:56:29.310
that we have been
detecting in our research,

00:56:29.310 --> 00:56:31.845
another kind of rhetorical strategy that

00:56:31.845 --> 00:56:33.661
we call trash talking democracy.

00:56:33.661 --> 00:56:36.810
Milan has heard about this (chuckles)

00:56:36.810 --> 00:56:40.408
where it's not so much you
don't badmouth the other party,

00:56:40.408 --> 00:56:43.074
you badmouth the institutions.

00:56:43.074 --> 00:56:48.074
So, you know, the courts
are full of corrupt actors,

00:56:48.240 --> 00:56:50.763
the press is incompetent.

00:56:52.710 --> 00:56:56.880
You know, the opposition and
the legislature are, you know,

00:56:56.880 --> 00:57:00.600
the legislature is, you know, unworkable.

00:57:00.600 --> 00:57:05.010
And so, the impact of that is to,

00:57:05.010 --> 00:57:07.470
we find just as an empirical matter

00:57:07.470 --> 00:57:11.664
to kind of tamp down any kind
of enthusiasm or optimism

00:57:11.664 --> 00:57:15.510
about institutions working.

00:57:15.510 --> 00:57:18.450
And that's also something
that I think is very,

00:57:18.450 --> 00:57:20.400
is sort of exaggerated
in the American context

00:57:20.400 --> 00:57:21.233
because we do this,

00:57:21.233 --> 00:57:22.980
this goes back to what Jacob was saying,

00:57:22.980 --> 00:57:24.652
we do have a whole, you know,

00:57:24.652 --> 00:57:27.360
big chunk of one of our
major political parties

00:57:27.360 --> 00:57:29.340
who are people who really
don't believe in government,

00:57:29.340 --> 00:57:31.191
and don't believe in public policy,

00:57:31.191 --> 00:57:36.191
and sort of make the failure
to provide good services,

00:57:38.910 --> 00:57:40.800
a kind of self-fulfilling function.

00:57:40.800 --> 00:57:44.220
There's a wonderful book
by an American sociologist

00:57:44.220 --> 00:57:47.520
Arlie Russell Hochschild about

00:57:47.520 --> 00:57:50.611
why is it the people in
cancer alley in Louisiana

00:57:50.611 --> 00:57:54.450
who understand perfectly well
that they're being poisoned

00:57:54.450 --> 00:57:57.750
by the petrochemical industry.

00:57:57.750 --> 00:57:59.040
Why did they support

00:57:59.040 --> 00:58:04.040
Tea Party anti-environmentalist lawmakers?

00:58:04.170 --> 00:58:06.360
And one of the reasons is that they see

00:58:06.360 --> 00:58:08.940
that even at the state
level that, you know,

00:58:08.940 --> 00:58:12.001
the kind of EPA equivalent
in the state of Louisiana

00:58:12.001 --> 00:58:13.680
does a terrible job.

00:58:13.680 --> 00:58:15.000
They don't get anything done.

00:58:15.000 --> 00:58:17.400
They don't actually protect them.

00:58:17.400 --> 00:58:19.519
Of course, that's a result of defunding,

00:58:19.519 --> 00:58:23.729
and you know, a kind of
starving of the institution.

00:58:23.729 --> 00:58:26.164
But then that becomes evidence that

00:58:26.164 --> 00:58:31.164
there's no point in electing,
you know, environmentalists.

00:58:31.230 --> 00:58:33.720
Representatives are
trying to push agencies

00:58:33.720 --> 00:58:38.040
to do a better job because
you have before you,

00:58:38.040 --> 00:58:41.283
you know, evidence that these
institutions don't work.

00:58:43.470 --> 00:58:45.030
- Thanks, Sue.

00:58:45.030 --> 00:58:47.520
Milan, do you have any comments?

00:58:47.520 --> 00:58:52.020
- Maybe just some comments on
this institutional question.

00:58:52.020 --> 00:58:55.013
So, I don't see much of a, you know,

00:58:55.013 --> 00:58:58.440
I don't see clear evidence
favoring or not favoring a system

00:58:58.440 --> 00:59:03.150
with party primaries over a
system without party primaries.

00:59:03.150 --> 00:59:06.570
But what strikes me as
being directly relevant

00:59:06.570 --> 00:59:10.260
to democratic erosion is the fact that

00:59:10.260 --> 00:59:13.470
the very tight two-party
system that exists

00:59:13.470 --> 00:59:14.610
in the United States

00:59:14.610 --> 00:59:17.400
creates a very stark
choice for voters, right?

00:59:17.400 --> 00:59:20.040
So, if one party's proposing a candidate

00:59:20.040 --> 00:59:22.038
that has authoritarian tendencies,

00:59:22.038 --> 00:59:25.500
but at the same time, represents
in a meaningful sense,

00:59:25.500 --> 00:59:28.320
also the policy preferences of voters,

00:59:28.320 --> 00:59:30.540
then a supporter of
those policy preferences

00:59:30.540 --> 00:59:31.421
faces a tough choice.

00:59:31.421 --> 00:59:34.650
They might vote for a
candidate who is democratic,

00:59:34.650 --> 00:59:37.560
but on the other side,
politically as in advocating

00:59:37.560 --> 00:59:39.458
for policies that they disagree with,

00:59:39.458 --> 00:59:43.380
or they must put up with a
candidate who is, you know,

00:59:43.380 --> 00:59:45.120
violating democratic norms,

00:59:45.120 --> 00:59:47.257
but also, happens to advocate
for the kind of policies

00:59:47.257 --> 00:59:48.090
that they like.

00:59:48.090 --> 00:59:51.840
And this is a very stark binary choice

00:59:51.840 --> 00:59:54.150
that's in effect a product
of the two-party system.

00:59:54.150 --> 00:59:55.369
And that in most places,

00:59:55.369 --> 00:59:58.333
which have multiple parties
does not have to be as stark

00:59:58.333 --> 01:00:00.330
because in addition, let's say,

01:00:00.330 --> 01:00:02.550
to a conservative who is authoritarian,

01:00:02.550 --> 01:00:04.110
there will be also a party that also

01:00:04.110 --> 01:00:05.700
has conservative leanings

01:00:05.700 --> 01:00:08.970
but is perfectly compliant
with democratic principles.

01:00:08.970 --> 01:00:13.635
And so, in some sense,
democracy has a very high price

01:00:13.635 --> 01:00:16.464
in the United States
for a subset of voters

01:00:16.464 --> 01:00:19.230
who face these kind of trade-offs.

01:00:19.230 --> 01:00:21.150
And so, in terms of
institutional solutions,

01:00:21.150 --> 01:00:23.026
I'm kind of very much interested in

01:00:23.026 --> 01:00:28.026
what's going to come
out of the ranked choice

01:00:28.412 --> 01:00:30.570
voting experiments,
(Alan speaks faintly)

01:00:30.570 --> 01:00:32.550
which still in the US
function in the context

01:00:32.550 --> 01:00:34.770
of the two major parties.

01:00:34.770 --> 01:00:38.700
But because they do not have
a stark diversion pressures,

01:00:38.700 --> 01:00:41.880
they do not face voters
who may have somewhat

01:00:41.880 --> 01:00:44.032
eclectic combination of preferences.

01:00:44.032 --> 01:00:46.461
They may not be aligned exactly

01:00:46.461 --> 01:00:48.300
the way the two major parties are.

01:00:48.300 --> 01:00:49.860
They may be sincerely voting

01:00:49.860 --> 01:00:50.880
for their first preference

01:00:50.880 --> 01:00:53.883
without having to sacrifice their vote.

01:00:56.790 --> 01:00:57.690
- Great, thank you.

01:00:57.690 --> 01:00:59.610
I mean, I guess, another issue related

01:00:59.610 --> 01:01:00.840
to what you were saying, Milan,

01:01:00.840 --> 01:01:03.120
is that when you have two parties,

01:01:03.120 --> 01:01:06.030
if you trash the other party,

01:01:06.030 --> 01:01:09.240
it's a sum zero game.

01:01:09.240 --> 01:01:11.550
Whereas if there are multiple parties,

01:01:11.550 --> 01:01:14.190
trashing the other party
may or may not read down

01:01:14.190 --> 01:01:16.440
to your benefit to the same extent.

01:01:16.440 --> 01:01:17.910
- I think that would be
the interaction effect

01:01:17.910 --> 01:01:21.000
between Sue's comments and my comments.

01:01:21.000 --> 01:01:23.473
- Excellent, thank you. Wonderful.

01:01:23.473 --> 01:01:27.570
Next question, to what extent, if any,

01:01:27.570 --> 01:01:30.330
might the effects of disinformation,

01:01:30.330 --> 01:01:32.970
apart from the effects
of objective conditions

01:01:32.970 --> 01:01:35.220
such as inequality affect your analysis?

01:01:35.220 --> 01:01:36.991
So, I guess, the general question

01:01:36.991 --> 01:01:41.410
is to what extent is
the erosion of democracy

01:01:42.510 --> 01:01:45.600
affected by the information
environment that,

01:01:45.600 --> 01:01:47.910
you know, we're now experiencing?

01:01:47.910 --> 01:01:51.060
- I mean, I should say I've
mentioned right-wing media,

01:01:51.060 --> 01:01:53.160
and media in general a number of times.

01:01:53.160 --> 01:01:56.160
So, I'll just emphasize
that again and say that,

01:01:56.160 --> 01:01:58.710
one way that inequality has factored in,

01:01:58.710 --> 01:02:02.137
or at least this is what
Paul and I argued in our book

01:02:02.137 --> 01:02:06.338
"Let them Eat Tweets,"
is that that inequality

01:02:06.338 --> 01:02:11.338
by creating essentially tensions

01:02:12.000 --> 01:02:13.337
for both parties coalitions,

01:02:13.337 --> 01:02:15.660
but particularly, for
the Republican coalition

01:02:15.660 --> 01:02:17.460
with its longstanding ties to business,

01:02:17.460 --> 01:02:21.630
but it's increasing reliance
on working class white votes.

01:02:21.630 --> 01:02:23.681
For that party to try to shift

01:02:23.681 --> 01:02:28.042
the focus of conflict from
kind of first dimension

01:02:28.042 --> 01:02:32.002
material size of government
issues towards more

01:02:32.002 --> 01:02:34.560
what sometimes called
second dimensions issues

01:02:34.560 --> 01:02:36.900
around race cultural conflict.

01:02:36.900 --> 01:02:38.186
And I just wanna be clear,

01:02:38.186 --> 01:02:41.501
if there's a lot of
second dimension appeals

01:02:41.501 --> 01:02:43.369
made by Democrats as well,

01:02:43.369 --> 01:02:46.039
but I think it's really
important to see how

01:02:46.039 --> 01:02:48.202
that strategy, right?

01:02:48.202 --> 01:02:50.373
Has been both.

01:02:51.540 --> 01:02:54.030
So, that's not a direct effect
of inequality, if you will.

01:02:54.030 --> 01:02:55.320
It's an effective inequality

01:02:55.320 --> 01:02:57.611
that's mediated by political strategies,

01:02:57.611 --> 01:02:59.220
competition between the parties.

01:02:59.220 --> 01:03:04.170
John Roemer, our colleague, now retired,

01:03:04.170 --> 01:03:07.282
made a argument along
these lines many years ago,

01:03:07.282 --> 01:03:08.704
which is basically if you introduce

01:03:08.704 --> 01:03:10.590
these second dimensions of conflict,

01:03:10.590 --> 01:03:12.780
then you're gonna get
these cross class parties.

01:03:12.780 --> 01:03:14.280
They're gonna be cleaved much more

01:03:14.280 --> 01:03:16.477
around cultural and social issues.

01:03:16.477 --> 01:03:19.140
And with the Republican
party in particular,

01:03:19.140 --> 01:03:21.960
there's just no question
that that strategy, right?

01:03:21.960 --> 01:03:23.490
Which took the objective change,

01:03:23.490 --> 01:03:26.139
but then translated into
a certain kind of response

01:03:26.139 --> 01:03:28.353
has been greatly furthered by the rise

01:03:28.353 --> 01:03:31.260
of a distinctive
right-wing media ecosystem.

01:03:31.260 --> 01:03:33.900
And so, it starts as Talk radio,

01:03:33.900 --> 01:03:37.470
it runs up into and through Fox News,

01:03:37.470 --> 01:03:39.180
which now is a moderate player

01:03:39.180 --> 01:03:42.480
in some of the right-wing media circles.

01:03:42.480 --> 01:03:45.450
There has been a ton of
research on this that shows

01:03:45.450 --> 01:03:47.499
that there is a lot of disinformation

01:03:47.499 --> 01:03:51.409
and misinformation in this
more isolated ecosystem.

01:03:51.409 --> 01:03:53.595
Perhaps the most persuasive argument

01:03:53.595 --> 01:03:55.150
that I've seen about this,

01:03:55.150 --> 01:03:56.580
because of course,

01:03:56.580 --> 01:03:59.160
these can become like
essentialist arguments,

01:03:59.160 --> 01:04:01.110
like, somehow, the right wing is gonna be,

01:04:01.110 --> 01:04:02.940
is more prone to dishonesty.

01:04:02.940 --> 01:04:05.070
But actually, the best argument I've seen

01:04:05.070 --> 01:04:07.530
is just the nature of the
ecosystems themselves.

01:04:07.530 --> 01:04:11.310
So, the left of center
ecosystem is kind of broad one

01:04:11.310 --> 01:04:13.860
that includes a lot of
mainstream media, right?

01:04:13.860 --> 01:04:15.940
It's just much more likely to police

01:04:16.800 --> 01:04:21.800
very inaccurate and extreme stuff, right?

01:04:21.930 --> 01:04:24.900
Right-wing media system is
a more self-contained unit.

01:04:24.900 --> 01:04:28.953
That's true both in terms
of how these media nodes

01:04:28.953 --> 01:04:30.780
kind of interact with each other

01:04:30.780 --> 01:04:32.790
as Yochai Benkler has shown in his work.

01:04:32.790 --> 01:04:33.623
But it's also true in

01:04:33.623 --> 01:04:36.086
who actually consumes the media, right?

01:04:36.086 --> 01:04:38.504
So, if you're someone
who dabbles a little bit

01:04:38.504 --> 01:04:40.110
in this range of media,

01:04:40.110 --> 01:04:42.458
which is true of a lot of
people in the center, right?

01:04:42.458 --> 01:04:44.580
Then, you're gonna get
exposed to a bunch of stuff.

01:04:44.580 --> 01:04:46.549
If you look at the people,
particularly the most strong

01:04:46.549 --> 01:04:48.333
supporters of kind of,

01:04:49.410 --> 01:04:52.157
we've sometimes talked to 'em
about them today as you know,

01:04:52.157 --> 01:04:55.034
MAGA, Make America
Great Again, candidates,

01:04:55.034 --> 01:04:57.420
those folks are much
more likely to congregate

01:04:57.420 --> 01:04:59.250
among the most conservative media sources

01:04:59.250 --> 01:05:00.688
where they're not
necessarily getting exposed

01:05:00.688 --> 01:05:02.550
to some of that contrary information.

01:05:02.550 --> 01:05:06.651
So, again, I just think
it's really important

01:05:06.651 --> 01:05:11.388
that we understand that our
institutions are distinctive.

01:05:11.388 --> 01:05:14.030
And also, if you look across countries,

01:05:14.030 --> 01:05:15.900
there are very few countries,

01:05:15.900 --> 01:05:18.104
I think Israel is really the major example

01:05:18.104 --> 01:05:20.147
that you see in the
cross national research

01:05:20.147 --> 01:05:22.290
where there's so much distrust,

01:05:22.290 --> 01:05:23.760
particularly among conservatives

01:05:23.760 --> 01:05:25.680
of mainstream media sources,

01:05:25.680 --> 01:05:28.530
and where there's so
much reliance on a kind

01:05:28.530 --> 01:05:32.850
of distinctive brand of
media of bipartisans.

01:05:32.850 --> 01:05:34.605
- Great. Milan, and Sue.

01:05:34.605 --> 01:05:37.440
Do you have anything to add about

01:05:37.440 --> 01:05:39.753
the communication environment?

01:05:43.465 --> 01:05:44.580
I can't tell if Milan,

01:05:44.580 --> 01:05:46.710
are you looking to get rid of your mute?

01:05:46.710 --> 01:05:47.583
Or are you?

01:05:49.615 --> 01:05:52.170
- I apologize. No, I don't
think I have anything to say.

01:05:52.170 --> 01:05:54.930
- Okay. Sue, do you have
anything you wanna add?

01:05:54.930 --> 01:05:56.355
- No, I agree with.

01:05:56.355 --> 01:05:59.163
- Terrific. Okay, question.

01:06:00.420 --> 01:06:03.600
Thank you all for your informative talks.

01:06:03.600 --> 01:06:05.097
Okay, I agree with that.

01:06:05.097 --> 01:06:06.150
Go. Okay.

01:06:06.150 --> 01:06:08.520
What are some actions that can be done

01:06:08.520 --> 01:06:11.063
or are already being done to counteract

01:06:11.063 --> 01:06:12.963
the erosion of democracy?

01:06:21.540 --> 01:06:22.373
Sue.

01:06:23.516 --> 01:06:26.324
Actually, Sue, could you
also say just a word about

01:06:26.324 --> 01:06:30.840
what Bright Line Watch is?

01:06:30.840 --> 01:06:34.320
'Cause, I think people who
are interested in this topic

01:06:34.320 --> 01:06:37.172
might be interested in learning
more about that as well

01:06:37.172 --> 01:06:38.263
as your answer.
- Yeah, for sure.

01:06:38.263 --> 01:06:40.147
- 'Cause, I think, it's
relevant to the question of,

01:06:40.147 --> 01:06:43.080
you know, what can you do
about the erosion of democracy?

01:06:43.080 --> 01:06:44.963
Is a public that's more alert

01:06:44.963 --> 01:06:47.377
to the the fact that there are threats

01:06:47.377 --> 01:06:50.670
to our democracy is
probably part of the answer

01:06:50.670 --> 01:06:51.603
to that question.

01:06:52.560 --> 01:06:54.981
- So, there, you know,

01:06:54.981 --> 01:06:58.873
I think participation is super important.

01:06:58.873 --> 01:07:03.180
So, thinking about what we can
do as individuals who may be,

01:07:03.180 --> 01:07:05.010
you know, students, or professors,

01:07:05.010 --> 01:07:07.113
or people you know, working in, you know,

01:07:08.130 --> 01:07:11.403
non-official kinds of jobs.

01:07:12.239 --> 01:07:16.320
Participation is very
important if, you know,

01:07:16.320 --> 01:07:21.188
Milan is completely right, that
we have all kinds of biases

01:07:21.188 --> 01:07:24.096
and tendencies to turn a blind eye

01:07:24.096 --> 01:07:27.686
to undemocratic behaviors
by our own parties.

01:07:27.686 --> 01:07:32.335
But to the extent that it
becomes really crucial,

01:07:32.335 --> 01:07:35.281
and I think we are really at a crossroads

01:07:35.281 --> 01:07:40.281
in our country right now
to sort of, you know,

01:07:40.380 --> 01:07:43.855
recommit to democracy as a
kind of system of government,

01:07:43.855 --> 01:07:46.680
and set of principles,

01:07:46.680 --> 01:07:48.903
and make that a kind of central point

01:07:48.903 --> 01:07:51.244
to your own participation,

01:07:51.244 --> 01:07:54.483
and the way you talk to
other people about politics,

01:07:55.380 --> 01:07:58.470
separate from the kinds
of public policy issues

01:07:58.470 --> 01:08:00.120
that we all care a lot about.

01:08:00.120 --> 01:08:02.070
Many of us care very passionately about

01:08:02.070 --> 01:08:04.050
public policy issues.

01:08:04.050 --> 01:08:07.393
But separate from those kinds of concerns,

01:08:07.393 --> 01:08:12.393
I think that there is a real
need to sort of think hard

01:08:12.849 --> 01:08:15.376
and talk hard, you know,
talk with people about

01:08:15.376 --> 01:08:17.250
what is it that democracy does,

01:08:17.250 --> 01:08:20.490
and what does it look like
it doesn't do very well.

01:08:20.490 --> 01:08:23.486
And you know, I talked a moment ago about

01:08:23.486 --> 01:08:28.486
this kind of phenomenon of
eroding backsliding leaders

01:08:29.160 --> 01:08:32.412
having a strategy of
denigrating democracy.

01:08:32.412 --> 01:08:34.317
Not so much democracy writ large,

01:08:34.317 --> 01:08:36.720
they usually say it's a
good system of government,

01:08:36.720 --> 01:08:40.410
but these particular
institutions really are hollow,

01:08:40.410 --> 01:08:42.360
and corrupt, and not worth it.

01:08:42.360 --> 01:08:44.220
So, don't worry about
it when I attack them.

01:08:44.220 --> 01:08:48.246
So, you know, I think
there's a dialogue to have

01:08:48.246 --> 01:08:50.358
with many different kinds of people.

01:08:50.358 --> 01:08:54.570
I personally feel a strong
need to have a dialogue

01:08:54.570 --> 01:08:58.050
with the left on this
and with young people.

01:08:58.050 --> 01:09:01.358
And I started my own household
where I have, you know,

01:09:01.358 --> 01:09:04.014
teenagers who are skeptical for sure,

01:09:04.014 --> 01:09:08.437
and who are quick to pick up
on the failures of democracy,

01:09:08.437 --> 01:09:11.010
even when it works pretty well.

01:09:11.010 --> 01:09:12.300
So, we have all kinds of, you know,

01:09:12.300 --> 01:09:14.880
so, the way to defend democracy
is not to whitewash it,

01:09:14.880 --> 01:09:19.090
is not to say it has worked very well

01:09:20.539 --> 01:09:22.290
in the history of the United States.

01:09:22.290 --> 01:09:24.565
It has had all kinds of biases,

01:09:24.565 --> 01:09:28.890
and has institutionalized
many elements of racism,

01:09:28.890 --> 01:09:30.510
and sexism, and all kinds of things

01:09:30.510 --> 01:09:33.210
that we want to overcome.

01:09:33.210 --> 01:09:35.962
Nevertheless, if you study the experiences

01:09:35.962 --> 01:09:38.881
of other countries where
people lose the ability

01:09:38.881 --> 01:09:43.470
to kick the government out
of office by voting them out,

01:09:43.470 --> 01:09:44.970
that is not a good place to be.

01:09:44.970 --> 01:09:48.494
It's a place to be where civil
rights are not respected,

01:09:48.494 --> 01:09:51.540
where the rights to
protest are not respected,

01:09:51.540 --> 01:09:53.220
where freedom of speech is not respected.

01:09:53.220 --> 01:09:57.049
So, I think that a kind of thinking hard

01:09:57.049 --> 01:10:00.425
about the difference
between a sharp critical

01:10:00.425 --> 01:10:04.205
constructive eye toward our
own political institutions,

01:10:04.205 --> 01:10:05.962
as opposed to, you know,

01:10:05.962 --> 01:10:07.637
the kind of institutional nihilism

01:10:07.637 --> 01:10:11.377
that many political
leaders are only too happy

01:10:11.377 --> 01:10:13.800
to encourage us to embrace.

01:10:13.800 --> 01:10:15.550
That's an important starting point.

01:10:16.920 --> 01:10:19.689
- And could you just say a
quick word about project.

01:10:19.689 --> 01:10:20.790
- (speaks faintly) Much, yeah.

01:10:20.790 --> 01:10:25.395
So, in late '20, when was it?

01:10:25.395 --> 01:10:29.447
I guess, it was coming up
to the election in 2016.

01:10:29.447 --> 01:10:31.910
A lot of economists got very upset

01:10:31.910 --> 01:10:35.825
because candidate Trump
was raising questions

01:10:35.825 --> 01:10:38.321
about the veracity and reliability

01:10:38.321 --> 01:10:42.164
of official government
statistics on things

01:10:42.164 --> 01:10:44.349
like unemployment rate.

01:10:44.349 --> 01:10:46.560
And they wrote a public letter

01:10:46.560 --> 01:10:49.260
that was signed by many former, you know,

01:10:49.260 --> 01:10:51.141
Nobel laureates and former presidents

01:10:51.141 --> 01:10:53.435
of the American Economics
Association so forth,

01:10:53.435 --> 01:10:54.517
that said,

01:10:54.517 --> 01:10:57.294
"Hey, you know, where would we be without

01:10:57.294 --> 01:10:59.748
the Bureau of Labor statistics?

01:10:59.748 --> 01:11:02.070
Where would we be without
these institutions?"

01:11:02.070 --> 01:11:05.354
It's not a good thing to question

01:11:05.354 --> 01:11:07.331
these very important institutions,

01:11:07.331 --> 01:11:12.331
and question sort of the
scientific basis of economics.

01:11:14.880 --> 01:11:17.465
And I remember I was having a conversation

01:11:17.465 --> 01:11:20.220
with our colleague,

01:11:20.220 --> 01:11:21.900
Gresham Helmke at the
University of Rochester

01:11:21.900 --> 01:11:23.407
and sort of very smugly saying,

01:11:23.407 --> 01:11:24.630
"Oh, these economists,

01:11:24.630 --> 01:11:26.700
they sure wait till the
last minute to do this."

01:11:26.700 --> 01:11:28.237
And then, it was then, she sort of said,

01:11:28.237 --> 01:11:30.210
"Hey, have we done anything like this?"

01:11:30.210 --> 01:11:33.060
So, we circulated a letter
of great concern about,

01:11:33.060 --> 01:11:36.330
you know, the threat that you know,

01:11:36.330 --> 01:11:37.560
certain parts of the discourse,

01:11:37.560 --> 01:11:40.030
and sort of, you know,
attacks on the press

01:11:41.160 --> 01:11:43.500
that point verbal attacks
on the press attacks on,

01:11:43.500 --> 01:11:46.140
you know, very virulent
attacks on political opponents,

01:11:46.140 --> 01:11:50.630
on immigrants, and so forth,
that seemed to represent

01:11:50.630 --> 01:11:55.435
a potential threat to
democratic institutions

01:11:55.435 --> 01:11:56.670
in the United States.

01:11:56.670 --> 01:11:59.495
And that elicited a very,
very strong response.

01:11:59.495 --> 01:12:03.310
We had, I think, every, almost
every living past president

01:12:03.310 --> 01:12:06.608
of the American Political
Science Association

01:12:06.608 --> 01:12:10.360
and just scores of our
colleagues signed the letter.

01:12:10.360 --> 01:12:11.558
And after the election,

01:12:11.558 --> 01:12:13.636
we thought, okay, we should do something.

01:12:13.636 --> 01:12:16.676
And the first thing we decided to do

01:12:16.676 --> 01:12:20.034
was to put together an
email list of our colleagues

01:12:20.034 --> 01:12:21.390
around the country,

01:12:21.390 --> 01:12:24.106
and get them to tell us what they think

01:12:24.106 --> 01:12:25.838
matters for democracy.

01:12:25.838 --> 01:12:27.990
So, we have, as those
of you who filled out

01:12:27.990 --> 01:12:29.130
these long surveys,

01:12:29.130 --> 01:12:30.990
now, they're in about
27 different dimensions

01:12:30.990 --> 01:12:32.580
that we talk about what is democracy?

01:12:32.580 --> 01:12:35.184
And then, that same set of dimensions,

01:12:35.184 --> 01:12:36.540
you know, how are we doing?

01:12:36.540 --> 01:12:38.040
How's the US doing on these things?

01:12:38.040 --> 01:12:41.997
And we've branched out and
done other kinds of questions,

01:12:41.997 --> 01:12:43.380
and other kinds of studies.

01:12:43.380 --> 01:12:44.490
But that's the basic.

01:12:44.490 --> 01:12:46.155
So, it's a collaboration between

01:12:46.155 --> 01:12:48.551
Dartmouth University of Rochester,

01:12:48.551 --> 01:12:51.663
and was Yale, now University of Chicago.

01:12:53.430 --> 01:12:55.611
- Great. Thank you very much, Sue.

01:12:55.611 --> 01:13:00.426
Again, the question, what are
some actions that can be done

01:13:00.426 --> 01:13:02.670
or are already being done

01:13:02.670 --> 01:13:05.700
to counteract the erosion of democracy?

01:13:05.700 --> 01:13:09.063
Milan, is there something
you'd like to add to that?

01:13:13.108 --> 01:13:17.850
- Maybe, briefly to
characterize what I think

01:13:17.850 --> 01:13:20.648
is the main force that is, you know,

01:13:20.648 --> 01:13:23.300
that acts in, you know,
to defend democracy.

01:13:23.300 --> 01:13:26.820
And I think that main
force are the voters.

01:13:26.820 --> 01:13:28.937
I think as voters as
being the ultimate check

01:13:28.937 --> 01:13:33.060
on any possible politician
that undermines democracy.

01:13:33.060 --> 01:13:35.660
And I think when we think
about what kind of voters,

01:13:38.070 --> 01:13:40.743
the kind of study that
I highlighted earlier,

01:13:42.030 --> 01:13:44.068
I've tried to do several
of those around the world

01:13:44.068 --> 01:13:46.920
and basically, try to
look at what are the kind

01:13:46.920 --> 01:13:49.890
of characteristic of the kind of voter

01:13:49.890 --> 01:13:51.573
that when the kind of party or candidate

01:13:51.573 --> 01:13:53.638
that they would normally vote for

01:13:53.638 --> 01:13:55.590
says something undemocratic

01:13:55.590 --> 01:13:57.448
or proposes something undemocratic,

01:13:57.448 --> 01:14:00.097
that that voter is willing to say,

01:14:00.097 --> 01:14:03.272
"I'm not voting for you just
because of that." Right?

01:14:03.272 --> 01:14:04.646
And I think,

01:14:04.646 --> 01:14:08.187
there are two actionable
predictors of that kind of voter

01:14:08.187 --> 01:14:10.080
or correlates of that kind of voter.

01:14:10.080 --> 01:14:11.005
And the first type is,

01:14:11.005 --> 01:14:12.232
well, that kind of voter tends

01:14:12.232 --> 01:14:14.329
to be a centrist or moderate, right?

01:14:14.329 --> 01:14:17.089
So, they are not hard line partisans

01:14:17.089 --> 01:14:19.905
because those are unwilling
to abandon the candidates

01:14:19.905 --> 01:14:21.150
that they otherwise like.

01:14:21.150 --> 01:14:24.390
So, in other words, centrist
are a pro-democratic force.

01:14:24.390 --> 01:14:25.590
But the other thing that's even,

01:14:25.590 --> 01:14:27.900
that I think is more actionable
is that these voters tend

01:14:27.900 --> 01:14:29.490
to be fairly political informed

01:14:29.490 --> 01:14:31.470
and democratically competent.

01:14:31.470 --> 01:14:34.156
They seem to be fairly
good at telling apart

01:14:34.156 --> 01:14:35.910
undemocratic from democratic,

01:14:35.910 --> 01:14:38.490
but also knowing basic political facts.

01:14:38.490 --> 01:14:40.500
And I think, so, I think, if there's hope,

01:14:40.500 --> 01:14:44.790
it's the hope in this kind of
voter as serving as a check.

01:14:44.790 --> 01:14:47.160
- Right, so, it it's not causal exactly,

01:14:47.160 --> 01:14:50.580
it's an association, but it makes sense.

01:14:50.580 --> 01:14:54.314
- It's an association and
it's one only of about four

01:14:54.314 --> 01:14:58.950
or five robust predictors
across about 10 countries.

01:14:58.950 --> 01:15:00.810
And so, I think that that's
the kind of surprising thing

01:15:00.810 --> 01:15:02.610
is like, regardless of
whether we're talking about

01:15:02.610 --> 01:15:05.520
Serbia, Sweden, or the United States,

01:15:05.520 --> 01:15:08.097
this is something that strongly
correlates with the kind

01:15:08.097 --> 01:15:10.447
of voter who's willing to say,

01:15:10.447 --> 01:15:12.810
"If it's my side that's
behaving undemocratically,

01:15:12.810 --> 01:15:15.360
I'm willing to switch
and vote for someone else

01:15:15.360 --> 01:15:18.510
precisely because they're
behaving undemocratically."

01:15:18.510 --> 01:15:20.977
- Milan, is this work
part of the APSR paper

01:15:20.977 --> 01:15:23.220
that you publish with matter?

01:15:23.220 --> 01:15:24.921
Or is it some other?

01:15:24.921 --> 01:15:27.044
- This is not published.

01:15:27.044 --> 01:15:28.920
This is not published.
- Okay.

01:15:28.920 --> 01:15:30.240
All right, so, this is breaking news.

01:15:30.240 --> 01:15:32.429
- But it's based on
those studies, basically.

01:15:32.429 --> 01:15:35.958
Looking now at the correlates
of the kind of voter

01:15:35.958 --> 01:15:37.620
that sides with democracy.

01:15:37.620 --> 01:15:39.658
- Okay, but for people who
are interested in reading more

01:15:39.658 --> 01:15:41.861
about what you've been talking about,

01:15:41.861 --> 01:15:45.014
the study that you showed excerpts from,

01:15:45.014 --> 01:15:48.210
that's an American Political
Science Review paper

01:15:48.210 --> 01:15:50.430
with Matt Graham from 2022.

01:15:50.430 --> 01:15:52.333
So, there is a paper there,

01:15:52.333 --> 01:15:56.490
although, this one, which I
showed is also a working paper.

01:15:56.490 --> 01:15:57.323
- This one?
- Yeah.

01:15:57.323 --> 01:16:00.960
This one is more focusing
on partisan mistrust.

01:16:00.960 --> 01:16:01.793
- Great, thank you.

01:16:01.793 --> 01:16:05.190
Jacob, is there something
you'd like to add?

01:16:05.190 --> 01:16:07.590
- Yeah, just, I'd just like to say first,

01:16:07.590 --> 01:16:09.355
Bright Line Watch is a great organization.

01:16:09.355 --> 01:16:13.680
I'm so pleased that it's out there,

01:16:13.680 --> 01:16:16.054
and I, at least, think
it has a presence at Yale

01:16:16.054 --> 01:16:18.933
because there's a lot of us
who admire it and draw in it.

01:16:20.250 --> 01:16:23.462
And I think that if I could
bring these comments together,

01:16:23.462 --> 01:16:25.080
I'd say two things.

01:16:25.080 --> 01:16:28.290
One is, yes, voters are kind
of the ultimate corrective

01:16:28.290 --> 01:16:31.170
and democratic system, but
the voters need help, right?

01:16:31.170 --> 01:16:32.648
Both, they need institutions

01:16:32.648 --> 01:16:36.000
that allow them to have
an effective voice,

01:16:36.000 --> 01:16:37.500
and they need the kind of information

01:16:37.500 --> 01:16:39.768
and support to be able to exercise that.

01:16:39.768 --> 01:16:42.434
And so, there I come
back to this idea that

01:16:42.434 --> 01:16:45.665
there's a real need for
building up organizations,

01:16:45.665 --> 01:16:49.140
broad, kind of civic
organizations of the sort that,

01:16:49.140 --> 01:16:49.987
you know, used.

01:16:49.987 --> 01:16:52.530
I think labor unions played
a very important role

01:16:52.530 --> 01:16:55.440
in this regard, as Sue said.

01:16:55.440 --> 01:16:56.406
But we also have,

01:16:56.406 --> 01:16:59.141
as Theta Scotch Bulls
work has suggested, right?

01:16:59.141 --> 01:17:00.540
We had a large number

01:17:00.540 --> 01:17:02.550
of kind of mass based civic organizations,

01:17:02.550 --> 01:17:04.080
some of them fraternal organizations,

01:17:04.080 --> 01:17:07.096
some of them veterans groups
that were much more active

01:17:07.096 --> 01:17:09.340
in sort of mobilizing citizens,

01:17:09.340 --> 01:17:11.880
and reforming them around broad issues.

01:17:11.880 --> 01:17:14.820
And I would say that,

01:17:14.820 --> 01:17:17.800
I am short-term very
worried as I've made clear,

01:17:17.800 --> 01:17:20.640
but I have a kind of long-term optimism

01:17:20.640 --> 01:17:24.060
and it rests on a couple things.

01:17:24.060 --> 01:17:26.465
One is that, you know, as we've discussed,

01:17:26.465 --> 01:17:29.089
there's a bunch of ideas
out there for reform, right?

01:17:29.089 --> 01:17:31.230
It's really what's the
challenge right now?

01:17:31.230 --> 01:17:32.871
It's like the political,

01:17:32.871 --> 01:17:35.700
it's like lifting yourself up
by your own bootstraps, right?

01:17:35.700 --> 01:17:37.530
The political system
isn't working very well.

01:17:37.530 --> 01:17:39.960
How do we fix the political
system in those circumstances?

01:17:39.960 --> 01:17:41.631
But we kind of know the formula

01:17:41.631 --> 01:17:45.150
and I'm really pleased that you, Alan,

01:17:45.150 --> 01:17:46.770
had created democracy innovations,

01:17:46.770 --> 01:17:48.060
which is thinking through

01:17:48.060 --> 01:17:49.920
some of these really innovative ideas

01:17:49.920 --> 01:17:51.930
for how to improve the performance of.

01:17:51.930 --> 01:17:53.010
No, actually, I think,

01:17:53.010 --> 01:17:54.480
how do we get through this period

01:17:54.480 --> 01:17:56.760
where we can't actually
pursue those ideas?

01:17:56.760 --> 01:18:00.000
We can experiment, we can think
about state level changes.

01:18:00.000 --> 01:18:03.960
But I also think, as Milan
was suggesting, right?

01:18:03.960 --> 01:18:07.530
We can get engaged and
if there's one thing

01:18:07.530 --> 01:18:10.830
that I think Bright Line Watch
does that organizations do,

01:18:10.830 --> 01:18:15.830
that Milan's stronger democrats
in the electorate do, right?

01:18:16.560 --> 01:18:18.364
Is they try to focus attention on

01:18:18.364 --> 01:18:20.175
what the stakes are, right?

01:18:20.175 --> 01:18:22.110
That's a little bit of what we're doing,

01:18:22.110 --> 01:18:24.360
but at least, I don't have illusions about

01:18:24.360 --> 01:18:25.860
how many people are listening.

01:18:26.940 --> 01:18:29.460
Well, I can see right
now, 108 of you out there.

01:18:29.460 --> 01:18:30.630
Thank you for listening.

01:18:30.630 --> 01:18:33.510
So, and hopefully, you have
lots of friends you can talk to,

01:18:33.510 --> 01:18:35.763
but I think one of the
things that's happened

01:18:35.763 --> 01:18:37.226
is we've kind of normalized

01:18:37.226 --> 01:18:40.960
pretty fundamentally
anti-democratic stances.

01:18:40.960 --> 01:18:44.070
And we need to essentially say that

01:18:44.070 --> 01:18:47.703
these are not appropriate.

01:18:47.703 --> 01:18:50.640
And that's gotta be part of our debates.

01:18:50.640 --> 01:18:53.066
I actually think it's striking that

01:18:53.066 --> 01:18:57.450
in the last two big elections.

01:18:57.450 --> 01:19:01.560
Actually, probably if you
go back to 2018 too, right?

01:19:01.560 --> 01:19:03.060
There's been a lot of hand wringing,

01:19:03.060 --> 01:19:06.840
and in the end where there's
been salience of the state

01:19:06.840 --> 01:19:08.580
about the stakes, right?

01:19:08.580 --> 01:19:11.670
Where there's candidates
who are really out there

01:19:11.670 --> 01:19:15.602
denying elections where, you
know, very fundamental rights

01:19:15.602 --> 01:19:18.450
that people care about are at stake.

01:19:18.450 --> 01:19:20.567
We've generally, not exclusively,

01:19:20.567 --> 01:19:23.766
we've seen greater engagement, right?

01:19:23.766 --> 01:19:27.179
And we've seen a lot of
positive breakthroughs

01:19:27.179 --> 01:19:30.180
for shoring up those institutions, right?

01:19:30.180 --> 01:19:32.600
And so, I think that's the hope, right?

01:19:32.600 --> 01:19:34.500
Is that you get that going,

01:19:34.500 --> 01:19:35.515
that kind of positive cycle

01:19:35.515 --> 01:19:38.117
rather than the vicious cycle going.

01:19:38.117 --> 01:19:41.820
And I believe over the long term we can.

01:19:41.820 --> 01:19:43.470
- Excellent. Thank you so much, Jacob.

01:19:43.470 --> 01:19:46.226
And so, I see that we're at 5:23.

01:19:46.226 --> 01:19:49.430
I do wanna say thank you
for the call out Jacob

01:19:49.430 --> 01:19:54.090
to the ISPS Democratic
Innovations Program.

01:19:54.090 --> 01:19:57.120
We have a lot of events that
are planned in the coming

01:19:57.120 --> 01:20:02.108
several months, including
a fairly large event,

01:20:02.108 --> 01:20:05.007
a conference event on rank choice voting,

01:20:05.007 --> 01:20:07.020
and we're having an event

01:20:07.020 --> 01:20:10.080
on the use of lotteries and
representational systems.

01:20:10.080 --> 01:20:12.510
And all sorts of things designed to try

01:20:12.510 --> 01:20:17.510
and think of systems that
would promote honest,

01:20:17.791 --> 01:20:20.203
efficient, and effective government.

01:20:20.203 --> 01:20:24.726
And I urge everyone who's
interested in such things

01:20:24.726 --> 01:20:28.890
to check out the ISPS
website for more information.

01:20:28.890 --> 01:20:32.430
So, with that, why don't
we see are there any final,

01:20:32.430 --> 01:20:35.686
like, literally, 30 to 45 second comments

01:20:35.686 --> 01:20:39.690
that you'd like panelists to make.

01:20:39.690 --> 01:20:42.194
And if there's any resources or papers

01:20:42.194 --> 01:20:45.261
or books that you think
would be especially important

01:20:45.261 --> 01:20:47.540
for people to read who are
interested in the subject,

01:20:47.540 --> 01:20:50.100
could you share those as well?

01:20:50.100 --> 01:20:53.343
We're kind of getting close to
the end of our time together.

01:20:54.420 --> 01:20:56.370
So, why don't we start in reverse order

01:20:56.370 --> 01:20:57.450
from the presentation.

01:20:57.450 --> 01:21:01.053
So, Milan, Sue, and then Jacob, please.

01:21:02.280 --> 01:21:03.330
- Maybe very briefly,

01:21:03.330 --> 01:21:06.873
I would say that what
I want to keep up with,

01:21:08.220 --> 01:21:12.480
what political scientists and
what the public think about

01:21:12.480 --> 01:21:14.100
the state of the US democracy.

01:21:14.100 --> 01:21:16.650
I keep up with the website
of the Bright Line Watch

01:21:16.650 --> 01:21:19.440
and especially, their reports on,

01:21:19.440 --> 01:21:23.370
I'm really fascinated by both
how much actually agreement

01:21:23.370 --> 01:21:25.440
there is between political scientists

01:21:25.440 --> 01:21:28.290
and ordinary voters about many things,

01:21:28.290 --> 01:21:31.260
but also, then the lines of disagreement.

01:21:31.260 --> 01:21:35.400
So, I find those periodic
studies incredibly valuable,

01:21:35.400 --> 01:21:38.977
and kind of, especially if
they're around major events

01:21:38.977 --> 01:21:40.419
in American politics, like,

01:21:40.419 --> 01:21:45.419
I see it as a social scientific
way to better understand

01:21:47.610 --> 01:21:48.900
the challenges to democracy.

01:21:48.900 --> 01:21:50.910
So, I really appreciate that.

01:21:50.910 --> 01:21:53.151
- Thank you, Sue.

01:21:53.151 --> 01:21:55.759
- Thank you. Thanks, Milan.

01:21:55.759 --> 01:22:00.759
One thing I would add is that
we actually have replicated

01:22:02.702 --> 01:22:05.977
the same set of questions
about dimensions of democracy,

01:22:05.977 --> 01:22:08.879
you know, what's important
in, I don't know,

01:22:08.879 --> 01:22:11.670
six or seven different
countries, very varied,

01:22:11.670 --> 01:22:14.676
Brazil, Poland, the UK, South Africa,

01:22:14.676 --> 01:22:16.140
bunch of different countries.

01:22:16.140 --> 01:22:19.380
And it's startling how
similar the results are.

01:22:19.380 --> 01:22:22.543
So, the view, you know,

01:22:22.543 --> 01:22:25.113
what really matters for democracy?

01:22:25.950 --> 01:22:27.300
What's essential?

01:22:27.300 --> 01:22:29.850
You know, people think
that equality of rights,

01:22:29.850 --> 01:22:32.460
of political rights, they
think that elect, you know,

01:22:32.460 --> 01:22:34.440
free and fair elections far and away

01:22:34.440 --> 01:22:35.640
the most important thing.

01:22:35.640 --> 01:22:37.440
And also, what doesn't matter so much.

01:22:37.440 --> 01:22:41.993
So, they actually, the votes
pope is that getting along

01:22:41.993 --> 01:22:45.480
and being nice to each
other isn't so important.

01:22:45.480 --> 01:22:47.790
I think, you know, like having legislators

01:22:47.790 --> 01:22:49.813
who, you know, go out to
dinner together to whatever,

01:22:49.813 --> 01:22:51.990
not that they don't think it's important,

01:22:51.990 --> 01:22:52.830
it's not as important.

01:22:52.830 --> 01:22:55.475
So, there's a lot of
conversions of the ideas about

01:22:55.475 --> 01:22:59.310
what democracy is internationally,

01:22:59.310 --> 01:23:03.649
I guess, I would just say
for folks who are interested

01:23:03.649 --> 01:23:05.469
in thinking about what you can do

01:23:05.469 --> 01:23:10.413
and less of our kind of
nerdy papers that we write.

01:23:11.400 --> 01:23:12.585
Another Yale professor,

01:23:12.585 --> 01:23:17.156
Tim Snyder in the history
department, his book on his,

01:23:17.156 --> 01:23:19.950
sort of really basically
pamphlet "On Tyranny"

01:23:19.950 --> 01:23:23.370
has a wonderful set of ideas about

01:23:23.370 --> 01:23:24.870
very concrete things you can do,

01:23:24.870 --> 01:23:27.209
like get subscriptions to newspapers

01:23:27.209 --> 01:23:29.383
and choose an organization
that you wanna champion,

01:23:29.383 --> 01:23:31.650
and help labor unions, and so on.

01:23:31.650 --> 01:23:35.250
So, I would take a look at Tim's book.

01:23:35.250 --> 01:23:36.541
- Great, thanks a lot, Sue.

01:23:36.541 --> 01:23:38.334
And then, Jacob, please.

01:23:38.334 --> 01:23:40.470
- Yeah, so first, if you didn't notice,

01:23:40.470 --> 01:23:41.430
'cause Sue was talking,

01:23:41.430 --> 01:23:43.080
I was holding up "On Tyranny,"

01:23:43.080 --> 01:23:45.169
which is right next to my desk.

01:23:45.169 --> 01:23:46.530
But I was also gonna mention

01:23:46.530 --> 01:23:48.150
I'm part of the American Academy of Arts

01:23:48.150 --> 01:23:51.180
and Sciences Commission on
"Reimagining the Economy."

01:23:51.180 --> 01:23:53.340
And it's a successor to the,

01:23:53.340 --> 01:23:54.990
this is an embargoed copy,

01:23:54.990 --> 01:23:58.920
and the real thing
doesn't have the binder,

01:23:58.920 --> 01:24:02.130
but it's a successor
to a well-known report

01:24:02.130 --> 01:24:05.670
that they did on improving the
Health of American democracy.

01:24:05.670 --> 01:24:08.280
And I think, it is telling
that, first of all,

01:24:08.280 --> 01:24:10.260
you could get together a
very broad cross section

01:24:10.260 --> 01:24:12.420
of people from very wide range of fields.

01:24:12.420 --> 01:24:15.517
And they all agreed that our future

01:24:15.517 --> 01:24:18.117
and our democracy
depends on thinking about

01:24:18.117 --> 01:24:19.841
how our we can make our economy

01:24:19.841 --> 01:24:22.110
work better for more Americans.

01:24:22.110 --> 01:24:23.730
And that kind of brings us full circle

01:24:23.730 --> 01:24:27.060
to where both Sue and I began
that, you know, ultimately,

01:24:27.060 --> 01:24:31.551
like capitalist democracy has this,

01:24:31.551 --> 01:24:34.650
you know, among rich democracies
has this pretty impressive

01:24:34.650 --> 01:24:36.630
track record of resilience

01:24:36.630 --> 01:24:40.170
in the face of a lot of
very fundamental shocks.

01:24:40.170 --> 01:24:41.865
And it rests on this idea that,

01:24:41.865 --> 01:24:44.130
as Milan said, that voters have autonomy.

01:24:44.130 --> 01:24:46.410
It also rests on the prosperity

01:24:46.410 --> 01:24:49.260
that wealth well-regulated
markets can produce.

01:24:49.260 --> 01:24:52.595
And it's that, you know,
that complementarity

01:24:52.595 --> 01:24:56.550
as political scientists call
it, of capitalism, democracy,

01:24:56.550 --> 01:25:00.930
that with reforms to both
capitalism and democracy

01:25:00.930 --> 01:25:02.760
should get us forward into the future.

01:25:02.760 --> 01:25:03.892
- Great.
- And so that,

01:25:03.892 --> 01:25:05.785
I think it's really great that,

01:25:05.785 --> 01:25:06.727
and yes, I know,

01:25:06.727 --> 01:25:10.140
I think it's really great the
academy has put out a report

01:25:10.140 --> 01:25:12.690
for how we might think about that.

01:25:12.690 --> 01:25:14.730
- Fantastic. Thank you so much, Jacob.

01:25:14.730 --> 01:25:17.580
So, I wanna also thank our staff at ISPS.

01:25:17.580 --> 01:25:20.130
We have the most amazing
staff that allow us

01:25:20.130 --> 01:25:24.240
to do these events, especially
Pam Green, Rick Harrison,

01:25:24.240 --> 01:25:27.805
and Kira Wishard who did
all of the arrangements

01:25:27.805 --> 01:25:30.180
to make this event possible.

01:25:30.180 --> 01:25:31.803
I wanna thank our panelists,

01:25:32.910 --> 01:25:34.721
Sue Stokes from Chicago,

01:25:34.721 --> 01:25:38.940
Milan, and Jacob from Yale.

01:25:38.940 --> 01:25:39.890
And I also want to thank everyone

01:25:39.890 --> 01:25:42.220
who attended our talk today,

01:25:42.220 --> 01:25:45.360
for the wonderful questions they shared,

01:25:45.360 --> 01:25:47.841
and the interesting conversations

01:25:47.841 --> 01:25:50.040
those questions generated.

01:25:50.040 --> 01:25:52.020
So, with that, thank you very much.

01:25:52.020 --> 01:25:55.985
And I look forward to sharing
additional ISPS events

01:25:55.985 --> 01:25:57.955
in the future with all of you.

01:25:57.955 --> 01:25:59.343
Have a nice evening.

01:26:00.750 --> 01:26:01.583
- Thank you.

